\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Rally belies undercurrent of fear. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Tamora Vidaillet \par \uc2 1,001 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 12:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Most Asian markets clawed \par \par \uc2  back some of their recent losses on Wednesday following \par \par \uc2  relative calm on Wall Street but nerves remained frayed as \par \par \uc2  investors awaited America's response to last week's devastating \par \par \uc2  attacks. \par \par \uc2      Strong gains in some markets belied the tension beneath the \par \par \uc2  surface as U.S. President George W.Bush stepped up his campaign \par \par \uc2  for international support in the war on terrorism. \par \par \uc2      Indeed, traders were not only worried that a conflict could \par \par \uc2  erupt soon, they were also concerned the crisis could drag on \par \par \uc2  indefinitely leaving markets in a state of suspense. \par \par \uc2      Domestic factors also played their part, especially in \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at weakening the \par \par \uc2  yen for the second time this week boosted export related \par \par \uc2  stocks. \par \par \uc2      Moves by some Asian monetary authorities this week to ease \par \par \uc2  credit amid renewed global uncertainty helped brighten \par \par \uc2  sentiment in Japan, South Korea and Taiwan although economists \par \par \uc2  remained gloomy about the region's prospects in the \par \par \uc2  medium-term. \par \par \uc2      Hong Kong was the first to slash rates in line with \par \par \uc2  aggressive easing in the United States and Europe this week, \par \par \uc2  followed by Taiwan and South Korea. \par \par \uc2      The Bank of Japan announced late on Tuesday that it, too, \par \par \uc2  would relax its already super-loose monetary stance. \par \par \uc2      "Some countries have eased monetary policy to boost \par \par \uc2  sentiment but it will be a cushion rather than a cure," said \par \par \uc2  DBS market strategist Philip Wee. "It will help but there will \par \par \uc2  still be suffering and companies will go bust." \par \par \uc2  Although some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had benefited from the \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar's recent setback, economic fundamentals in Asia \par \par \uc2  still pointed to more regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness ahead, \par \par \uc2  analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There will be revisions to growth especially in the \par \par \uc2  (Asian) economies impacted by the electronics downturn," P.K. \par \par \uc2  Basu, chief economist at Credit Suisse First Boston told \par \par \uc2  Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2      "Growth numbers for exports and GDP will have to be slashed \par \par \uc2  even more for the final four months of this year." \par \par \uc2      Singapore, Taiwan and South Korea were likely to be the \par \par \uc2  worst affected, he said. \par \par \uc2      LOOSER CREDIT BOOSTS BONDS \par \par \uc2      Relatively small overnight losses on Wall Street compared \par \par \uc2  to Monday and concerted rate cuts fuelled interest in some \par \par \uc2  Asian bonds. \par \par \uc2      Korean sovereign bonds due in 2008 were quoted around 10 \par \par \uc2  basis points (bps) tighter compared to their New York close in \par \par \uc2  early Asian trade after the Bank of Korea cut rates at an \par \par \uc2  unscheduled meeting. \par \par \uc2      Malaysia's '11s were five bps tighter at 258 bps over \par \par \uc2  Treasuries. \par \par \uc2      Illiquidity and uncertainty had pushed spreads on \par \par \uc2  high-grade Asian bonds out by as much as 100 bps on Philippine \par \par \uc2  sovereigns and by at least 20 bps on other Asian benchmarks \par \par \uc2  after the aerial attacks on the United States which are feared \par \par \uc2  to have killed more than 5,000 people. \par \par \uc2  CREDIT EASING LIMITED FOR NOW \par \par \uc2  Certain Asian countries were unlikely to match concerted \par \par \uc2  easing elsewhere until fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, capital \par \par \uc2  flight or inflation had been allayed, according to some \par \par \uc2  economists. \par \par \uc2  The Philippines is one example of an Asian country where \par \par \uc2  there is an argument against rate cuts given weakness in its \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Philippines has really been \par \par \uc2  observing monetary policy based on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  direction," said \par \par \uc2  Thio Chin Loo, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2      "If they did cut there could be capital outflows and the \par \par \uc2  peso could suffer even more." \par \par \uc2      Other countries like Thailand and Indonesia could suffer \par \par \uc2  from similar concerns partly because events in the United \par \par \uc2  States could have accentuated existing investor unwillingness \par \par \uc2  to invest in Southeast Asian markets, she said. \par \par \uc2  But according to CSFB's Basu, an absence of official policy \par \par \uc2  response in Southeast Asia was partly because interest rates in \par \par \uc2  parts of the region had been lower than those in the United \par \par \uc2  States until only recently. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's Bank Negara still had room to ease rates by \par \par \uc2  around 25 basis points while Indonesia was likely to slash \par \par \uc2  rates once inflation had come off current levels of around 12 \par \par \uc2  percent, he said. \par \par \uc2  "I would expect (Indonesia) rates to come off substantially \par \par \uc2  in November or December, perhaps by as much as 200 basis \par \par \uc2  points." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     117.52       118.96         +1.23\par  Sing dlr      1.7419       1.7370         -0.28\par  Taiwan dlr    34.572       34.575         +0.01\par  Korean won   1295.50      1290.00         -0.42\par  Baht           44.17        44.29         +0.27\par  Peso           51.38        51.27         -0.21\par  Rupiah       9625.00      9098.00         -5.48\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     117.52       114.45         -2.61\par  Sing dlr      1.7419       1.7365         -0.31\par  Taiwan dlr    34.572       32.900         -4.84\par  Korean won   1295.50      1277.00         -1.43\par  Baht           44.17        43.70         -1.06\par  Peso           51.38        50.75         -1.23\par  Rupiah       9625.00      9470.00         -1.61\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.8000         +0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Market       Current      Prev Close     Pct Move\par  Tokyo        9939.60      9679.88         +2.68\par  Hong Kong    9558.15      9307.90         +2.69\par  Singapore    1345.06      1324.30         +1.57\par  Taipei       3781.17      3774.62         +0.17\par  Seoul         486.75       484.93         +0.38\par  Bangkok       283.03       274.21         +3.22\par  Manila       1187.05      1206.38         -1.60\par  Jakarta       421.84