\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia shares edge higher, but nerves taut. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 09:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Asian share markets carved out moderate gains on Wednesday for the second day in a row, but investors remained on edge, bracing for possible U.S. military retaliation for last week's devastating attacks. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said a well-coordinated round of global interest rate cuts provided underlying support but failed to allay fears that the attacks made a global recession all but inevitable - worries reflected in fresh losses on Wall Street on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Overall sentiment is still not very good, people are still worried that the market may retest its recent lows if the U.S. goes to war in retaliation," said Patrick Yiu, associate director at Kingsway Fund Management in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Bank of Japan stepped in to sell yen for the second time in a week, giving a modest boost to the sagging U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei blue-chip average closed 2.68 percent higher at 9,939.60 but failed to keep a toehold above the psychologically important 10,000 mark, regained during the morning for the first time since Friday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares were lifted by the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) decision late on Tuesday to relax its already super-loose monetary stance and by hopes that the government will finally prod banks to accelerate the disposal of bad loans that are choking the economy. \par \par \uc2  "There are expectations that Japan will probably contribute to the global policy alliance against terrorism by keeping its fragile economy from deteriorating," said Tetsuya Ishijima, chief strategist at Okasan Securities. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has taken its responsibility. So, what the government should do is to make progress in the nation's bad loan problem," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  EASING \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was just one of a raft of central banks that followed the rate-cutting lead set on Monday by the U.S. Federal Reserve and matched by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The central banks of South Korea and New Zealand joined the easing rush on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But Boeing Co's announcement of between 20,000 and 30,000 layoffs because of an expected fall in aircraft orders due to the deadly attacks merely reinforced expectations that easier credit will not be enough to avert a recession. \par \par \uc2  The emergency monetary easing failed to fire up U.S. equities, which were unable to hold on to early gains on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average ended 0.19 percent lower at 8,903.40, the lowest close since mid-December 1998, after failing to hold on to early gains, while the Nasdaq Composite Index fell 1.55 percent to 1,555.08, its lowest since mid-October 1998. \par \par \uc2  Although shares in Hong Kong and Korea rose on Wednesday, they fell in Singapore and Manila as uncertainty gripped investors. \par \par \uc2  "People right now don't want to move, there's no selling pressure and no buying too. They're just holding on to cash and waiting to see what would happen in the coming days...They're being very, very cautious," said Irving Ackerman, president of I. Ackerman & Associates Inc in Manila. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR VULNERABLE TO EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar remained vulnerable as investors scale back their exposure to the United States and start repatriating funds or spreading their risks by swapping into other markets. \par \par \uc2  "This process was, to some extent, under way prior to last week's unsavoury events but the attack and its likely repercussion of an accelerated economic slowdown has hastened the reduction in U.S. dollar asset exposure," National Australia Bank analysts said in a commentary. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, for the second time this week, pushed the U.S. dollar up towards 118 yen from 117.10 and also helped it regain some ground against the euro which traded around $0.9245. \par \par \uc2  "We are definitely not pursuing deflation and are in fact trying to push up prices. In order to do that, I think the most effective way is to lower the yen exchange rate," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said, explaining the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc a favourite safe-haven asset, also remained strong against the dollar, although it eased to 1.6035 per dollar from late New York highs near 1.5970. \par \par \uc2  In the bond markets, the round of rate cuts gave added impetus to Asian paper, already buoyed by a rally in emerging market bonds in New York. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing better bids as people are short-covering on benchmarks which were sold off very very dramatically in the last couple of days," a senior bond trader with a European investment bank said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010919dx9j007f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
