\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Japan's helping hand pushes dollar higher. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 08:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 19 (Reuters) - The dollar was given some breathing space on Wednesday as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for a second time this week to curb an export-damaging rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But while the dollar was yanked off earlier lows, it struggled to make much headway amid gloom over the fate of the U.S. economy, shaken after last week's suicide aircraft attacks that left more than 5,000 people dead or missing. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities seem quite determined to support the dollar, but there are more than a few who are seeing this as a great chance to unload dollars," a Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  By late afternoon, the dollar had drifted to 117.86/91 yen , having jumped a full yen from 117.10 as the Bank of Japan (BOJ) bought dollars for yen. That compared with a late offshore level of 117.24 yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen also moved lower against the euro, with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 109.04/09 yen from 108.50 yen, although the BOJ was said to have bought only dollars. \par \par \uc2  Against the dollar, the euro eased to 92.48/58 cents from 92.73 in late New York, but above Tuesday's low of around 92.05 cents. \par \par \uc2  BOJ DELIVERS \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the morning as the dollar slipped toward 117.00 yen, an apparent threshold the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also responded to in a dollar-buying move on Monday. \par \par \uc2  An international monetary source told Reuters in late afternoon trade that the BOJ was buying dollars for yen again around 0600 GMT. The dollar was then trading around 117.70 yen, and blipped up briefly to match the day's high of around 118.10. \par \par \uc2  Japan reiterated that a strong yen boded ill for the economy, with Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa saying that driving the yen lower was the best way to offset deflationary pressure. \par \par \uc2  But players said Japanese authorities were keen to defend a specific level rather than to reverse market direction. \par \par \uc2  "We can see the authorities buying time with rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Taisuke Tanaka, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "But we're faced with very negative factors for the dollar right now." \par \par \uc2  He cited possible capital flows from U.S. markets and frail U.S. consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  Fresh data on the U.S. economy on Tuesday confirmed that consumer spending has already been hit hard, with retail sales plunging in the wake of the attacks on symbols of American financial and military might. \par \par \uc2  Although a wave of rate cuts from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the world helped prevent the worst for financial markets after last Tuesday's attacks, analysts said they failed to soothe concerns over the shaky global economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were also concerned about the impact of possible U.S. retaliation as the White House kept up its verbal "war on terrorism". \par \par \uc2  "At best, we may see U.S. consumer confidence improving by the end of the year, but that will also depend on how the United States retaliates," Tanaka said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0616 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000