\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia opens stronger again, but fears remain. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Mair \par \uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 08:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Asian markets put on a brave face on Wednesday, opening stronger after Wall Street slipped to near three-year lows on fears of sagging corporate profits and a global recession. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Bank of Japan stepped in to sell yen for the second time this week, giving a boost to the sagging U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei blue-chip average rose 3.41 percent in morning trade, breaking above the psychologically important 10,000 mark for the first time since Friday as it shrugged off a 0.19 percent fall in the Dow Jones Industrial Average \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks were also firmer, but investors remained cautious after last week's attacks on Washington and New York as they waited to see how the United States would retaliate. \par \par \uc2  Even a global round of interest rate cuts was seen as doing little to hold off a global slowdown, illustrated by Boeing Co's announcement of between 20,000 and 30,000 layoffs because of an expected fall in aircraft orders due to the deadly attacks. \par \par \uc2  The central banks of South Korea and New Zealand cut interest rates on Wednesday, following a lead set on Monday by the U.S. Federal Reserve and matched by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Japan among others. \par \par \uc2  But the emergency easing has failed to fire up U.S. equities, which were unable to hold on to early gains on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Although the Dow ended just fractionally lower at 8,903.40, the close was the lowest since mid-December 1998, while the Nasdaq Composite Index fell 1.55 percent to 1,555.08, its lowest since mid-October 1998. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, stocks with close links to the U.S. market were shunned in favour of local issues such as dominant mobile phone operator NTT DoCoMo, which jumped 9.52 percent. "Telecoms and software developers attracted buyers as domestic demand-oriented high-tech issues. They are pretty much shielded from the recent international developments," said Katsuhiko Kodama, head of equities at Toyo Securities. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR VULNERABLE TO EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar remains vulnerable to the fall in equity markets, as investors scale back their exposure to the United States and start repatriating funds or spreading their risks by swapping into other markets. \par \par \uc2  "This process was, to some extent, under way prior to last week's unsavoury events but the attack and its likely repercussion of an accelerated economic slowdown has hastened the reduction in U.S. dollar asset exposure," National Australia Bank analysts said in a commentary. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, for the second time this week, pushed the U.S. dollar up towards 118 yen from 117.10 and also helped it regain some ground against the euro which traded around $0.9250. \par \par \uc2  "As I have said before, the ministry will take appropriate action if conditions warranted. Today's move is in line with our stance," Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told reporters soon after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc a favourite safe-haven asset, also remained strong against the dollar, although it too pulled back from its late New York highs near 1.5970 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Gold opened Asian trade at $287.50/8.00, in line with New York's closing levels but below London's late fix of $289.40, as traders waited for developments in the crisis. \par \par \uc2  Even debt, which can be a safe haven when equity markets are unsettled, has been caught in the confusion after the attacks. \par \par \uc2  While rate cuts and the threat of recession have reduced yields on short-dated bonds, yields on 30-year U.S. Treasury bonds have hit six-week highs on expectations that massive public spending pledged by Washington will increase the supply of government debt and boost the eventual economic recovery. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010919dx9j005vt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
