\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar resumes fall against major  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 14:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The dollar resumed its fall against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, as markets shrugged off a series of global rate cuts and focused on the risks of prolonged military conflict and a U.S.-led global recession. \par \par \uc2  Heavy stock losses in Europe despite early gains in Asia were making investors nervous of a further tumble on Wall Street, with U.S. stock futures pointing to a weak start. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shed as much as half a percent against the yen after the Bank of Japan joined other central banks in easing monetary policy to calm markets and boost growth following last week's attacks on the U.S. Traders said the BOJ's move could benefit the ailing domestic economy in the longer term. \par \par \uc2  "What we could see now is weak dollar sentiment dominating from the point of view that the U.S. economy will remain weak and there is heightened global uncertainty following last week's attacks," said Sonja Hellemann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  At 1215 GMT, the dollar stood near the day's lows around 117.15 yen, within one percent of Tuesday's seven-month lows. It pared early gains against the euro to trade around $0.9245, little changed on the day. \par \par \uc2  SWISSIE GETS THUMBS UP \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was the chief beneficiary of the increased uncertainty in global markets, with safe-haven flows pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to all-time highs around 1.4765 per euro. \par \par \uc2  It traded around 1.5980 Swiss francs per dollar, up about half a percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  "A continued reduction of exposure to global risk is helping the Swiss franc," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank. "General global uncertainty means the Swissie is benefiting from inflows." \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc has racked up gains of more than 1.5 percent against the euro since Friday when it was trading around 1.5030 per euro. \par \par \uc2  MORE CENTRAL BANKS TAKE ACTION \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan cut its discount rate to 0.10 percent from 0.25 percent. It added it was boosting its liquidity injection to the banking system by raising the target for funds overflowing from the money market into current account deposits at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  above the current six trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England, meanwhile, cut its key rate a quarter point, joining Monday's wave of rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster confidence and boost growth. \par \par \uc2  Reports earlier in the day that Afghanistan's ruling Taliban movement could launch a holy war against the United States if attacked, added to concerns over how the U.S. will respond to last week's assaults. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said market concerns were now refocusing on the state of the U.S. economy and, above all, the stock markets, which could still hit the dollar hard in the event of big falls. \par \par \uc2  They had expected stock market losses of between five and 10 percent on Monday and in the event the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average shed around seven percent. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  ON \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Monday to stem the yen's export-crimping strength and traders said markets remained wary of further Japanese action. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not eager to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but would do so if necessary. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill on Monday announced his support for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a stand which will probably keep alive talk of the potential for further dollar-buying by the Bank of Japan or even the United States. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, said meanwhile the BOJ's decision to ease monetary policy was appropriate. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010918dx9i00p5n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
