\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Rally tempered by Taliban holy war threat. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sabyasachi Mitra \par \uc2 1,234 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Some Asian equity markets fell back on Tuesday after Afghanistan's ruling Taliban threatened a holy war against the United States, denting an earlier rally in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  Japan, Asia's biggest stock market, saw its bellwether Nikkei average rack up gains of 1.85 percent, rebounding from 17-year lows hit on Monday while South Korea's benchmark share index rose 3.45 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei average, which was up four percent at one point, wiped most of its gains in late trade on news that police were investigating bomb threats at offices of foreign financial firms and at two top hotels in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Falls of around seven percent for the Dow Jones and Nasdaq and of about five percent for the S&P 500 on the first trading day after last week's devastating attacks on the United States were well within market expectations. \par \par \uc2  In points terms, the Dow's fall was its largest ever. But percentage-wise it did not make the top 10 steepest daily drops, dwarfed by the record 22.6 percent fall of Black Monday, October 19, 1987. \par \par \uc2  A call by the Taliban for a holy war against the U.S. if Washington attacked Kabul, underscored the uncertainty looming over global financial markets and rattled investors, despite efforts by authorities to calm down markets. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks erased morning gains to end lower after reports of Taliban's call. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are still worried about the implications of the U.S. going to war, so people are using every opportunity to take profits," said Ben Kwong, associate director at brokers KGI Asia. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index ended down 0.12 percent after rising more than three percent at one point in the morning session following a 50 basis point cut in U.S. interest rates and the smooth resumption of trading on the New York Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  Similar reversals were seen in other markets in Asia. Malaysia's benchmark share index abandoned gains to breach the psychological 600 points level, but later recovered to close down 0.22 percent at 607.64 points. \par \par \uc2  "This is serious and people will only care about getting out," said Lee Heng Guie, chief economist at HLG Capital. "There is no sense to it, no forecast, investors just want cash," he added. Singapore shares also reversed the course sharply in afternoon trade, giving up morning gains. \par \par \uc2  The key Straits Times Index was down 0.76 percent. The index was up as much as 2.0 percent in the morning, boosted by U.S.-led rate cuts by central banks. \par \par \uc2  RATE CUTS BRING SHORT-TERM RELIEF \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve cut its key short-term interest rate to three percent just before Wall Street reopened from its longest closure since the Great Depression, and made it clear it was prepared to do so again if necessary to keep the economy growing. \par \par \uc2  The Fed was matched by the Bank of Canada, and then the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a half point cut that it said was coordinated with the United States. The Swiss National Bank quickly followed the ECB. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong, where the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar, and Taiwan followed the U.S. Fed with cuts in its key interest rates while the Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would convene a special monetary board meeting. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, whose interest rates are already at zero, was also expected to ease its ultra-loose monetary policy stance on Tuesday in a bid to revive an economy teetering on the brink of its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR NOTCHES UP GAINS \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose almost half a percent against the euro on Tuesday, supported by rate cuts by major central banks and relief that U.S. stock losses defied the worst predictions. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  held about one percent above Monday's seven-month lows against the yen with markets wary of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap yen strength. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Tokyo was not eager to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  but would do so if necessary. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill on Monday announced his support for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a stand which will likely keep alive talk of the potential for further dollar-buying by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) or even the United States. \par \par \uc2  INVESTORS SEEK HIGH GRADE ASIAN BONDS \par \par \uc2  The coordinated rate cuts by central banks breathed life into the Asian dollar bond market as investors made a cautious return to trade. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market focus will be on interest rates and on expectations of statements by central banks on transmission of U.S. rate cuts," Raja Visweswaran, head of Asian credit research at Bank of America in Hong Kong, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and traders expect the Asian yield curve to steepen substantially as investors seek the safe havens of short dated sovereigns and high-grade bonds. \par \par \uc2  Asian bonds were well bid early on Tuesday after a stable overnight performance from emerging market debt in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The tone is better and the market here is better bid by five to 10 basis points," said a senior bond trader at a European bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Spreads on South Korean sovereign bonds due 2008 tightened by six basis points (bps) to 170 bps over comparable U.S. Treasuries against an overnight close of 176 bps in New York trade. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. $0913 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Japan yen     117.90       117.23         -0.57\par   Sing dlr      1.7376       1.7300         -0.44\par   Taiwan dlr     34.55        34.58         +0.07\par   Korean won   1297.00      1298.50         +0.12\par   Baht           44.20        43.98         -0.50\par   Peso           51.35        51.25         -0.19\par   Rupiah       9540.00      9300.00         -2.52\par   Ringgit       3.7995       3.7995         +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.90       114.45         -2.93\par   Sing dlr      1.7376       1.7365         -0.06\par   Taiwan dlr     34.55        32.90         -4.78\par   Korean won   1297.00      1277.00         -1.54\par   Baht           44.20        43.70         -1.13\par   Peso           51.35        50.75         -1.17\par   Rupiah       9540.00      9470.00         -0.73\par   Ringgit       3.7995       3.7997         +0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   ASIAN STOCK MARKETS  0913 GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Change on the day\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Market       Current      Prev Close     Pct Move\par   Tokyo        9679.88      9504.41         +1.85\par   Hong Kong    9307.90      9319.35         -0.12\par   Singapore    1324.30      1334.45         -0.76\par   Taipei       3774.62      3952.49         -4.50\par   Seoul         484.93       468.76   