\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar claws higher as central banks comfort. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chisa Fujioka \par \uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 05:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The dollar clawed higher on Tuesday as central banks around the world trimmed rates in a bid to calm worries over the U.S. economy following last week's air attacks. \par \par \uc2  Although U.S. share prices fell sharply on Monday - the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average posted its biggest point-loss ever - analysts said it could have been much worse. \par \par \uc2  And while uncertainty surrounds the direction of U.S. stocks, the dollar would be supported on monetary easing steps and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't say the pressure on the dollar has eased," said Masayuki Yamamoto, research analyst at Bank of America in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "But it's holding up for now on the possibility the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, along with the likelihood it will ease monetary policy further." \par \par \uc2  In late morning, the dollar held at 117.93/98 yen from 117.68 in late New York and up from a 6-= month trough of 116.65 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro wavered at $0.9215, backing off from a six-month high of $0.9335 overnight, while the Australian dollar suffered at five-month lows at US$0.4970. \par \par \uc2  RATE CUTS \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve, attempting to avert a U.S. recession after the attacks, slashed interest rates by half a percentage point just before Wall Street opened after a four-day shutdown. \par \par \uc2  The Fed was quickly followed by rate cuts by the Bank of Canada, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Swiss National Bank and Sweden's Riksbank. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expected further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "With conditions likely to worsen still further in the U.S. and global economy, further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action will be required," said Robert Rennie, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  "Thus, it seems likely that the Fed will cut again at the October 2 meeting. And other central banks are likely to cut in the meantime," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan (BOJ), which kicked off a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday, was widely expected to ease already ultra-loose credit, although Governor Masaru Hayami said a decision would not be made before Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have said that if the BOJ were to ease, it could either raise the current account deposit target or increase outright buying of government bonds from the market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  The dollar was also propped higher on speculation that Japanese monetary authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to cap a yen rise, especially after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill welcomed Japan's solo foray into the market on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ stepped in to buy dollars for yen for the first time since April 2000, a move O'Neill said was appropriate for the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated on Tuesday Japan would act again in the market when necessary. \par \par \uc2  But Bank of America's Yamamoto doubted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have a lasting impact, with the dollar likely to suffer from damage to U.S. consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would benefit most from weaker dollar sentiment. The Swiss franc has soared on safe-haven demand in uncertainty following the attacks. \par \par \uc2  But others argued that European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be free from worries about the fallout from the attacks, given the large exposure of European insurance companies. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has not been bought that much since Monday," Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I think concerns that capital will escape from the U.S. to Europe have eased, at least for now. The central banks have given the first aid," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010918dx9i00d5u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
