\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ set to ease policy after global rate cuts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara and Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 07:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked set to to ease its already ultra-loose monetary policy on Tuesday after its European and U.S. counterparts cut interest rates to counter instability triggered by the terror attacks on America. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan said it would end its two-day policy meeting a day early on Tuesday, after government ministers lined up to urge a suitable response to rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  But while the early decision made some kind of easing almost certain, analysts said that with rates already near zero the BOJ's options were limited. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said his representative will ask the BOJ Policy Board to further loosen its quantitative easing stance. \par \par \uc2  "Senior Vice Finance Minister (Seiichiro) Murakami will ask for further monetary easing...that is the Finance Ministry's position," he told a news conference. "He will also state that the BOJ should stem price declines." \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's government has been pressuring the BOJ to do its part to help boost the flagging economy, which appeared to be heading back into recession even before last Tuesday's shattering events in America. \par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said the BOJ could not afford to do nothing in the wake of the concerted monetary easing by the U.S. Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We have entered a new phase of cooperative monetary policy...monetary easing on a global scale is being required" \par \par \uc2  He said that the government would compile a list of requests on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy later in the day. Representatives from the Finance Ministry and the Cabinet Office can attend BOJ's policy meetings and submit policy requests but they cannot vote. \par \par \uc2  HAYAMI SAYS NO EARLY MOVE \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami had earlier denied any plan to announce the decision on Tuesday, but some analysts had expected it as a way of showing the BOJ's resolve to stand shoulder-to-shoulder with its foreign counterparts. \par \par \uc2  In an emergency meeting on Monday, the Fed cut both the key Federal funds rate target and the more symbolic official discount rate by 50 basis points shortly before the U.S. stock market resumed trade for the first time since September 10. \par \par \uc2  The ECB quickly followed suit by unexpectedly slashing its key short-term interest rates by half a percentage point. \par \par \uc2  Aside from the mounting worries about global recession after the deadly attacks in New York and Washington, Japan faces a tricky period as companies and banks close their books for the six months to September 30. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday, just two weeks before the book closings, Mycal Corp, Japan's fourth-largest supermarket chain operator, filed for court protection from creditors, leading to fears that many suppliers and creditor banks could take a hit. \par \par \uc2  Expectations of further BOJ action sent Japan's key 10-year government bond yield to a one-month low of 1.32 percent on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  GIVING AWAY MONEY \par \par \uc2  But just how the bank will ease policy further is unclear. \par \par \uc2  Japanese interest rates are already near zero and yet demand for virtually free money has remained disappointingly slack. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said the BOJ should expand its quantitative easing rather than cut short-term interest rates. Asked whether the BOJ should maintain current account deposits at eight trillion yen ($68 billion), he said: "I think so." \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, in tandem with other central banks, has been supplying ample liquidity to prevent financial markets from seizing up after last week's attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  As a result, current account deposits, the BOJ's reserves target, have risen to around eight trillion yen, some two trillion yen more than its current target of six trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  "This is just an emergency rise, but the BOJ could say it will be a constant measure," said Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Ishii. \par \par \uc2  Another option would be to use yen funds from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pump more liquidity into the banking system. Shortly after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday to buy dollars as the greenback fell to seven-month lows below 117 yen, the BOJ suggested it might not re-absorb the yen funds it used, leaving the market with extra liquidity. \par \par \uc2  But analysts were largely sceptical about how effective these policy options would be. \par \par \uc2  "If they cut the discount rate or increased reserve targets by a minimal amount, then I think it makes no difference to the economy or the financial markets," said Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Barings Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "They might, however, change tack more significantly and for example make some commitment to buying private sector assets or perhaps more interestingly extend funding to the RCC (Resolution and Collection Corp)." \par \par \uc2  The RCC is a state-backed body helping to clean up banks' chronic bad loan problems by taking on their bad debt. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010918dx9i00ac9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
