\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia relieved that Wall Street plunge not worse. \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Mair \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 06:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Repeats, changing slug from ATTACK-MARKETS-GLOBAL) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 18 (Reuters) - Asian markets opened higher on Tuesday, relieved that Wall Street's biggest-ever points plunge was not worse and buoyed by a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts, but the spectre of global recession could not be shaken. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 684.81 points, or 7.13 percent, to a near three-year low of 8,920.70 on the first trading day since last Tuesday's devastating attacks, but markets across Asia responded with stronger openings, led by a 3.22 percent morning rise in Japan's Nikkei index. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were acutely aware the global outlook was still very uncertain and the risks were weighted towards recession, despite the best efforts of U.S. officials to talk up the economy. \par \par \uc2  "I think we're in for a rocky ride today, but overall we should stay in positive turf on the back of somewhat surprising gains in Europe, a relatively benign fall for the Dow, and vague hopes the Bank of Japan will join the cutting fray," said Masaru Kazama, head of equities at Nissan Securities. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people had short positions that they rushed to cover given last night's developments." \par \par \uc2  GLOBAL RATE CUTS \par \par \uc2  The Federal Reserve cut its key short-term interest rate by half a percentage point before Wall Street reopened after its longest closure since the Great Depression, and made it clear it was prepared to do so again if necessary to keep the economy growing. \par \par \uc2  The Fed was matched by the Bank of Canada, and then the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced a half-point cut that it said was coordinated with the United States. The Swiss National Bank quickly followed the ECB. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, which started a two-day Policy Board meeting on Tuesday, is also expected to ease its already ultra-loose monetary stance - although Governor Masaru Hayami said the decision would not come on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "An intermeeting cut by the Fed had been expected by many; nor was it particularly surprising to see other central banks cut in an effort to offset the latest damage to global economic confidence as a result of last Tuesday's terrible events," said Rory Robertson, Macquarie Bank strategist in New York. \par \par \uc2  The view that even before the attacks the U.S. economy needed a rate cut has intensified concerns of a global recession, but U.S. officials, led by President George W. Bush and Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill, did their best to talk up the economy. \par \par \uc2  "I've got great faith in the economy," Bush told reporters. "The underpinnings for economic growth are there." \par \par \uc2  U.S. DOLLAR OFF LOWS \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar pulled back from its multi-month lows against the yen and the euro, as the global easings and wariness of more Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tempered the urge to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  "I think concerns that capital will escape from the U.S. to Europe have eased, at least for now. The central banks have given the first aid," said Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The yen softened to over 118 to the dollar in morning trade, having hit a six-month low of 116.65 on Monday, while the euro was just above 92 cents, a cent below its six-month high of 93.35 in London. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also lost ground on Tuesday, trading back above 1.61 to the dollar after having hit an eight-month high near 1.5960 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The relief in markets also saw another safe-haven asset, gold relinquish some of Monday's gains as it opened down nearly $2 an ounce in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  ASIAN MARKETS STRONGER \par \par \uc2  In points terms Wall Street's fall was its largest ever. But in percentage terms it was not even in the Top 10 steepest daily drops, dwarfed by the record 22.6 percent fall of Black Monday, October 19, 1987. \par \par \uc2  "There were steep price falls, but that was to be expected," Robertson said. Indeed, the Dow's 7.13 percent fall was close to the the Nikkei's total loss of 7.66 percent since the attacks. \par \par \uc2  Asia's second-largest stock market, Hong Kong, opened up over two percent and extended its gain to 2.64 percent in late morning. \par \par \uc2  Australian stocks rallied three percent in early trade, but by early afternoon the advance had been pared to about 1.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  South Korea and Singapore were also up, but Taiwan's market was still closed because of Typhoon Nari. \par \par \uc2  "There's probably a little bit of relief after the U.S. open last night, but it's really going to be what happens over the next few weeks rather than the next few days," said fund manager Martin Littler at Commonwealth Investment Management in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  "The economy in the U.S. and globally is still in trouble, certain industries are in dire straits," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010918dx9i0073m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
