\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Econ Min Takenaka: BOJ Needs To Act Flexibly \b0\par\par\uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 03:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka said Tuesday that Japan and other governments have entered a new age of international cooperation on monetary policy, and the Bank of Japan needs to act flexibly in response to last week's terrorist attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "The fall by the Dow Jones Industrial Average was about what was expected, and the market absorbed the shock very well... The cooperative monetary policy moves worked miraculously," Takenaka said at a regular press conference. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's policy response is now of interest," he added. \par \par \uc2  Takenaka said he wants the BOJ to "act with mobility" in response to these events when its policy board begins a regular two-day meeting later today. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of Canada matched a rate cut by the U.S. Federal Reserve Monday as part of a global effort to calm markets in the wake of last Tuesday's terrorist attack. \par \par \uc2  The Fed lowered its key federal funds rate 50 basis points to 3%, while the ECB cut its main interest rate by the same amount to 3.75%. The Bank of Canada reduced its key rate to 3.5% from 4%. The central banks of Switzerland and Sweden made similar cuts. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are in a new age of international cooperation. It used to be that responses were made to domestic factors, but now they are also being made to international ones," Takenaka said. \par \par \uc2  Takenaka said he still plans to attend the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy board meeting Wednesday, though he is still adjusting his schedule for Tuesday afternoon and is uncertain if he will be able to attend the first part of the meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he thought the BOJ might act early and make a new policy decision Tuesday instead of on the second day of the policy board meeting as is normally the case, Takenaka said "that is up to the BOJ." \par \par \uc2  Usually the policy board analyzes data on the first day of its two-day meetings and makes policy decisions on the second day. \par \par \uc2  The government will decide on what position it will present at the meeting at around noon (0300 GMT) before the meeting begins, Takenaka said. While Takenaka and an appointed \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  observer do not have a vote in policy board decisions, they are entitled to express their views at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  He said however, he believes the BOJ also is "conscious" of the need to act in cooperation with other central banks. \par \par \uc2  Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy dollars for yen, meanwhile, showed Japan was acting together with other countries, Takenaka said. \par \par \uc2  "It showed a great deal of coherence with the rates cuts," Takenaka said. "MOF acted with very appropriate timing." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Audrey McAvoy, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par audrey.mcavoy@dowjones.com\par -0-\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka said\par Tuesday that Japan and other governments have entered a new age of\par international cooperation on monetary policy and the Bank of Japan needs\par to act flexibily in reponse to last week's terrorist attacks in the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The fall by the Dow Jones Industrial Average was about what was\par expected and the market absorbed the shock very well...The cooperative\par monetary policy moves worked miraculously," Takenaka said at a regular\par press conference.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japan's policy reponse is now of interest"\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Takenaka added he wants the BOJ to "act with mobility" in response to\par these evnts when its policy board begins a regular two-day meeting later\par today.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of Canada matched a rate cut by\par the U.S. Federal Reserve Monday as part of a global effort to calm\par markets in the wake of last Tuesday's terrorist attack.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Fed's lowered its key federal funds rate 50 basis points to 3%,\par while the ECB cut its main interest rate by 0.5 percentage point to\par 3.75%. The Bank of Canada reduced its key rate to 3.5% from 4%. The\par central banks of Switzerland and Sweden made similar cuts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think we are in a new age of international cooperation. It used to\par be that responses were made to domestic factors, but now they are also\par being made to international ones," Takenaka said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010918dx9i0003x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
