\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar jumps after  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 08:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar jumped on Monday after Japanese monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying dollars for yen in a move evidently aimed at bolstering confidence in both the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the global financial system. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after the dollar slipped to a fresh seven-month low of 116.65 yen, shaken by uncertainty over the U.S. economy in the wake of devastating terror attacks on the country's financial capital last week. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement he had taken the action because "the recent sharp appreciation of the yen could have undesirable implications for the recovery of Japan's economy". \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have made no secret of their desire to see a weaker yen to help their economy avoid recession. They have repeatedly warned they could act at any time, so their action on Monday was not a huge surprise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Considering exchange rate levels, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was no surprise. (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities) took the action they said they were going to take," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also be part of global efforts to defend the dollar in the aftermath of last week's attacks. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar hit a seven-month low against the yen because of the terror attacks. The authorities needed to avoid such disruptions in the economy," said Naoto Ohnuma, manager at Mitsubishi Corp. \par \par \uc2  "People are deeply worried where U.S. shares are going to go when trade resumes on Monday. The authorities needed to defend the dollar to ease worries about the global financial system." \par \par \uc2  There was plenty of speculation that major central banks could coordinate monetary easing to win back confidence in the world economy. The U.S. Federal Reserve is widely expected to cut interest rates by as much as half a percentage point before long. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan begins a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  FOLLOW THROUGH? The dollar was changing hands at 118.01/06 yen in the afternoon, up more than one yen from a morning bottom but still one yen below levels the same time last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose even more, firming against the dollar to 92.75 cents from around 92 cents in New York and to 109.36/39 yen from 107.84 yen \par \par \uc2  While the total size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not immediately clear, dealers said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been conducting a wave of dollar buying since lunch time. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the dollar's gains were limited because the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was relatively small. \par \par \uc2  "I would assume the goal right now is to bring some stability to the market, rather than to push the dollar up. I think that is probably it for the moment," said Ron Leven, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman brothers. "One hundred and seventeen seems to be their trigger point, and they would probably be reluctant to step in again before Wall Street opens later on," Leven added. \par \par \uc2  Others argued that the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was substantial, saying the dollar failed to climb further because dollar sales from speculators outweighed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  They said many speculators had likely been caught in dollar-long positions, with rating agency Standard and Poor's cutting its rating outlook for Japan immediately before the attacks. \par \par \uc2  They said authorities could continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and the U.S. Federal Reserve could also take action later in the day in a coordinated action, they said. \par \par \uc2  It would be the first such move since last September, when the Fed, BOJ and ECB made a joint effort to rescue the euro. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar's credibility deteriorates further in the very short term, I think joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will happen," Morgan Stanley's Umemoto said. "In that sense, today's U.S. equity markets will be the key." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010918dx9h00d4h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
