\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar seen holding its own in Asian trade. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 744 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 01:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 18 (Reuters) - The dollar is expected to hold up in Asian trade on Tuesday after central banks around the globe orchestrated rate cuts aimed at soothing concerns about the U.S. economy after last week's attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  Although U.S. share prices fell sharply, with the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average posting its biggest point loss ever, analysts said it could have been much worse. \par \par \uc2  "There is a sense of relief in the market," said Toshiyuki Suzuki, chief economist at Sanwa bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The Dow's fall of 7.31 percent was smaller than fall in European share markets since the attacks, Suzuki noted. \par \par \uc2  The Fed, attempting to avert a U.S. recession after the attacks, slashed interest rates by half a percentage point just before Wall Street opened after a four-day shutdown. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action was followed quickly by rate cuts by the Bank of Canada, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Swiss National Bank and Sweden's Riksbank. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan starts the first day of a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday and there is speculation it could also loosen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prop up the dollar, should it fall further against the yen. The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, pushing up the dollar by about one yen in the early afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ could not join the coordinated rate cuts because the rates are already zero, but it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market again, if the dollar fell below 117 yen," said a dealer at a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Robert Rennie, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac in Sydney, said he expected further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "With conditions likely to worsen still further in the U.S. and global economy, further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action will be required," Rennie said. \par \par \uc2  "Thus, it seems likely that the Fed will cut again at the October 2 meeting. And other central banks are likely to cut in the meantime," he said. \par \par \uc2  "In the spirit of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cohesion, it likely that we will see action from them (the BOJ)," he said. \par \par \uc2  BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  POSSIBLE \par \par \uc2  In early Asian trade, the dollar was steady near 117.74/79 yen , up more than a full yen from 6-= month bottom at 116.65 yen hit on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro dropped to 91.72 cents in New York trade, from six-month high of 93.35 hit in London. In early Asia, it stood at 92.47/50 cents, steady from late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has not been bought that much since Monday," Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I think concerns that capital will escape from the U.S. to Europe have eased, at least for now. The central banks have given the first aid," Ueno said. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc, which has soared on safe-haven demand in uncertainty following the attacks, was firm around 1.6046/51 francs to the dollar after rising a full point to hit a eight month high near 1.5960 francs. \par \par \uc2  But analysts also said European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be free from worries about the fallout from the attacks, given the large exposure of European insurance companies. \par \par \uc2  Although most analysts are still worried that the U.S. economy could deteriorate further and that U.S. share prices could fall further, they also said the authorities could continue its easing campaign if economic conditions look set to worsen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 2312 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                                 17 Sep.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9232   0.9243   -0.12    -2.78   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen               117.87   117.85   +0.02    +2.99   114.45\par  Euro/yen              108.83   108.94   -0.10    +0.11   108.71\par  Dlr/swiss             1.6064   1.6052   +0.07    +0.32   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr               1.4640   1.4655   -0.10    -2.40   1.5000\par  Can/dlr               1.5693   1.5698   -0.03    +5.22   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr               0.5010   0.5022   -0.24   -10.44   0.5594\par  Euro/swiss            1.4833   1.4838   -0.03    -2.55   1.5221\par  Euro/stg              0.6304   0.6305   -0.02    -0.33   0.6325.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h018g4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
