\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX \highlight\uc2  foray no prompt for broader  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 13:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying spree on Monday is unlikely to be copied by other central banks unless the dollar slides so fast as to destabilise U.S. asset markets, European analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Japan waded into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in a year on Monday, buying dollars for yen in a bid to halt the yen's export-crimping strength. \par \par \uc2  The last time major central banks made a joint foray into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was in September 2000, when the Group of Seven sought to prop up the ailing euro. Barring a freefall in the greenback, similar action to prop up the dollar is not expected. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's concern is with a strong yen not a weak dollar, so it is very much a domestic issue," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. authorities are unlikely to come in unless we see a precipitous slide in the dollar which jeopardises U.S. asset markets." \par \par \uc2  Japan warned it would act to cap further yen appreciation even before last week's terror attacks on the United States, fearing a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hurting its already fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan has the largest pool of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is no stranger to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since 1995, the bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  36 times to weaken the yen and nine times to strengthen it, according to Deutsche Bank research. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the United States has been more reluctant to meddle with market forces and the current administration has been at pains to talk up its free-market credentials. \par \par \uc2  HANDS-OFF APPROACH \par \par \uc2  Since taking office, U.S. President George W. Bush and Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill have both stated that markets should determine the level of the dollar - comments taken by most traders to mean the current administration is less keen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than its predecessor. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has welcomed the recent recovery of the euro against the dollar, would be reluctant to jeopardise this revival. \par \par \uc2  "The chance of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling euros for dollars is zero," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "The chance of any action from the Fed is also extremely unlikely unless the dollar falls, say, five or six percent in one day." \par \par \uc2  The dollar has fallen around three percent in trade-weighted terms since Tuesday's devastating aircarft attacks, bringing its losses from 15-year peaks scaled in early July to around eight percent. \par \par \uc2  Panic selling of the dollar was kept in check last week as world central banks poured liquidity into the markets and said they stood ready to take any necessary measures to limit market disruption. These comments fuelled speculation of coordinated interest rate cuts as well as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if fluctuations became excessive. \par \par \uc2  But dollar-selling resumed on Monday as dealers braced for heavy selling of U.S. stocks when the equity markets reopen. \par \par \uc2  ASSET REACTION \par \par \uc2  Foreign portfolio flows into riskier U.S. assets - notably equities and corporate bonds - have fuelled the dollar's bull-run over the last six years. \par \par \uc2  If overseas investors decide suddenly to bring home these funds, the dollar would be acutely vulnerable to concerns over the U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  The United States relies on foreign inflows worth more than $1 billion a day to fund its trade gap, which has ballooned to record levels in recent years. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of the dollar leading U.S. assets into a vicious downward spiral is something U.S. authorities would be anxious to avoid. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. authorities are more concerned with the functioning of the markets rather than the actual level of the dollar," said Russell Jones, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "Coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely, but given uncertainties on how U.S. asset markets will react, it cannot be ruled out." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h00n03\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
