\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar quivers before crucial Wall Street open. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Robin Shepherd \par \uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - World \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets braced for the reopening of Wall Street on Monday with fears of big U.S. equity losses driving the dollar down and sending investors streaming into the relative safe haven of the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  Jitters about the resumption of U.S. stock market trading at 1330 GMT were exacerbated by early falls on Europe's major bourses as well as movements in the Japanese Nikkei which closed down five percent to hit a new, 17 year low. \par \par \uc2  "There is real, nervous anticipation of the opening in U.S. equity markets. The reaction of the Swiss franc has been evidence of a growing risk aversion in world markets," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Asia, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BoJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen which had pushed up against the greenback to levels which officials feared may harm \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's already fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  served as a warning that excessive dollar bearishness could be punished, although they noted that it had been conducted for domestic reasons. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down by more than a percent against the euro and Swiss franc while the BoJ action had limited losses to around a third of a percent against the yen. \par \par \uc2  At 1000 GMT, it was hovering around six month lows versus the euro around $0.9300 and eight month lows against the Swiss franc at 1.5995 francs. \par \par \uc2  However, evidence that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market nervousness extended beyond the dollar came with the euro's descent to all time lows against the Swiss franc at 1.4880 francs. \par \par \uc2  "The Swiss franc is the big gainer overall. This is due to the general state of extreme nervousness about what is going to happen when Wall Street opens," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Volumes were said to be thin, around 50 percent down on a normal trading day, exposing the market to heightened volatility. \par \par \uc2  ALL EYES ON WALL STREET \par \par \uc2  U.S. equity markets have been closed since last Tuesday's devastating attacks, the longest shutdown since the Great Depression in 1933. \par \par \uc2  Equity analysts have no real idea what to expect although hotel, insurance and airline stocks are widely thought to be among the most vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  Economists say the Federal Reserve will be monitoring the situation closely and that immediate interest rate cuts were a possibility in the event of hefty losses. \par \par \uc2  There was also talk that major central banks could coordinate monetary easing to win back confidence in the world economy. The Fed was widely expected to cut interest rates by as much as half a percentage point before long in any case. The BOJ begins a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought dollars for yen after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below 117 yen, shaken by uncertainty over the U.S. economy in the aftermath of last week's destructive attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been undertaken because "the recent sharp appreciation of the yen could have undesirable implications for the recovery of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy". \par \par \uc2  While the total size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not immediately clear, dealers said the BoJ had been conducting a wave of dollar buying during the second half of Japanese trade. This continued into the start of the European session, according to an international monetary source. \par \par \uc2  "The BoJ's been alone overnight on dollar/yen and it's been the only buyer holding it up. But the authorities do not want a run on the dollar," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have repeatedly warned they could act at any time to cap the yen's strength, so their action on Monday did not come as a huge surprise to the market. \par \par \uc2  The Fed, BoJ and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  together in September 2000 in a joint bid to rescue a frail euro. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h00ku2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
