\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar eyes Wall Street open, weak across board. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 10:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar fell more than one percent against the euro and the Swiss franc on Monday as traders nervously awaited U.S. stock markets' return to business but won respite against the yen after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry, through the Bank of Japan, bought dollars for yen after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell below 117 yen, shaken by uncertainty over the U.S. economy in the aftermath of last week's destructive attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  Japan is concerned that a strong yen will damage its already fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  But the greenback set fresh six-month lows against the euro and eight-month troughs versus the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is under pressure across the board as the market is nervous ahead of the re-opening of U.S. stock markets," said Ian Stannard, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is purely on the yen side as the economy is in a precarious situation and there could be wider action if the dollar comes under excessive pressure," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was because "the recent sharp appreciation of the yen could have undesirable implications for the recovery of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy". \par \par \uc2  While the total size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not immediately clear, dealers said the BOJ had been conducting a wave of dollar buying during the second half of Japanese trade. This continued into the start of the European session, according to an international monetary source. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood around 117.50 yen around 0827 GMT, up almost one yen from earlier seven-month lows around 116.70 yen. It was mired at six-month troughs beyond $0.9330 per euro, down about 1.25 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  MORE TO COME? \par \par \uc2  The BOJ said that its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unsterilised. This means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from draining yen funds supplied to the market created through the yen-selling, thus allowing the yen to weaken further. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials have repeatedly warned they could act at any time to cap the yen's strength, so their action on Monday was not a huge surprise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the dollar's gains were limited because the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was relatively small. But others disagreed, saying the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was substantial and many speculators had likely been caught in long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  There was some talk the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be part of global efforts to defend the dollar in the aftermath of last week's plane attacks in the United States, with speculation the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and the U.S. Federal Reserve could also step in later in the day in a coordinated action. \par \par \uc2  The Fed, BOJ and ECB last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  together in September 2000 in a joint bid to rescue a frail euro. \par \par \uc2  However some analysts were sceptical of the chances of coordinated action. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's concern is with a strong yen not a weak dollar, so it is very much a domestic issue," said Steve Barrow at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "In my view, the chance of any other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, or even this week, is much less than even." \par \par \uc2  ALL EYES ON WALL STREET \par \par \uc2  Wall Street was expected to take centre stage, with the U.S. stock markets due to reopen later. Equity markets have been closed since last Tuesday's attacks, the longest shutdown since the Great Depression in 1933. \par \par \uc2  Jitters over how stocks will react to the attacks helped send the dollar to its lowest in eight months around 1.5957 Swiss francs. \par \par \uc2  Global stock markets also nervously anticipated the U.S. stocks open, with Japan's Nikkei stock index tumbling five percent to close at its lowest since December 1984. \par \par \uc2  There was talk that major central banks could coordinate monetary easing to win back confidence in the world economy. The Fed is widely expected to cut interest rates by as much as half a percentage point before long. The BOJ begins a two-day policy meeting on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very nervous, very illiquid. Bid/offer spreads are probably wider," said one London based trader. \par \par \uc2  "In fact, activity is much the same as at the end of last week. Everybody's waiting for Wall Street." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h00hli\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
