\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Japan acts to halt yen rise, buys dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 13:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Japan acted to halt a damaging rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, buying dollars for yen in the authorities' first market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly a year. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt an "undesirable" rise in the yen against the dollar, which came amid flagging confidence in the U.S. economy following last week's terror attacks on America. \par \par \uc2  "The recent sharp appreciation of the yen in the exchange market could have undesirable implications for the recovery of Japan's economy. In this context we have taken appropriate action today in the exchange market," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to closely monitor the market and take appropriate action as necessary for the stability of exchange rates," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa later told Reuters that Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which earlier had slumped to seven-month lows below 117 yen leaped to 118.00 against the yen in Tokyo after Japanese monetary authorities confirmed they had acted. \par \par \uc2  But in early European trade it had slipped back to 117.21/25 despite what dealers say may have been follow-up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF). \par \par \uc2  All eyes were on the reopening of the U.S. equity markets, closed since last Tuesday's devastating terror attacks in New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  The yen's rise since last week had raised worries it could tip Japan's already struggling economy back into recession, partly by hitting the profitability of its big exporters. \par \par \uc2  Many economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came as no great surprise and confirmed that officials had been paying close attention to the 117 yen level. \par \par \uc2  "Well, it worked. It pushed the dollar up a big figure," said Ron Leven, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "One hundred and seventeen seems to be their trigger point, and they would probably be reluctant to step in again before Wall Street opens later on," he said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely do little more than temporarily halt the dollar's slide. Its impact could be limited, given the uncertain political and economic outlook in the United States, they said. \par \par \uc2  "Given weak sentiment toward the United States following the terror attacks, I doubt that the current trend of a strong yen will be altered any time soon," said Kenji Arata, an economist at MCM Asia Pacific. \par \par \uc2  SMALL AMOUNT \par \par \uc2  Most market players said they were unsure of the total amount involved in Monday's action, but some speculated on a figure of around $100-300 million. \par \par \uc2  That is fairly small compared to the Japanese government's previous attempts to move the market, but trade has been unusually thin since last week's attacks and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may still be concerned about dollar settlement risk. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan later announced it would provide markets with ample liquidity, including the use of funds from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which implies the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unsterilised. \par \par \uc2  In sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbs all the yen funds it injects, whereas in unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the money is left in the financial system, which can indicate a move towards monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  The last time the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on September 22, 2000 when it bought euro for yen. It last bought dollars for yen on April 3, 2000. \par \par \uc2  The yen's retreat gave the stock market a small boost, lifting it from 17-year lows in afternoon trade as exporter shares were bought back. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h00aci\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
