\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ says will use unsterilised  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 142 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 07:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) said on Monday it will provide ample liquidity to the money market and even utilise funds from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , signalling it will use so-called unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the past, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has absorbed most, if not all, of the excess yen funds in the private-sector banking system resulting from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will provide ample liquidity, using funds including those from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to ensure smooth settlement and stability in financial markets," a BOJ spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 118 yen , up from seven-month lows below 117 yen, after the BOJ - under instruction from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - bought dollars for yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a rapid yen rise. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010917dx9h009ez\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
