\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr Outlook Remains Weak Despite Global Rate Cuts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,302 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 18:16\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar took heart Monday from a global round of interest rate cuts and a less-than-expected sell-off in U.S. stocks, clawing back some of its losses after last week's terrorist attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  But analysts argue that the longer-term outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains weak, given the deep shocks to the U.S. economy and to global investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  "Despite the Fed's cut, despite bravado from U.S. officials and other officials around the globe, you can't help but feel the situation for the dollar has deteriorated, not just because of last Tuesday, but in terms of how the economy had been developing anyway," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  Under normal circumstances, Monday would be a major day for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. First, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  made good on its oft-repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat, stepping into the market in Asian trade and buying dollars for yen in a bid to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave an immediate lift to the dollar, but the effect was short-lived. Then, at the start of the New York day, the Federal Reserve cut interest rates by 50 basis points in another bid to ensure markets continue to function and to provide U.S. stocks with a dose of badly-needed confidence. \par \par \uc2  The Fed's cut was followed later in the New York morning by a surprise 50 basis point cut by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as similar easings by the Bank of Canada and the Swiss National Bank. \par \par \uc2  Coupled with massive liquidity injections by central banks around the world since Tuesday's attacks, the rate cuts show a determined and coordinated effort by Group of Seven nations to minimize market carnage and ward off major damage to already-slowing global economies. \par \par \uc2  In making its surprise move, the ECB noted that "following the terrorist attacks on the U.S.,  uncertainty about the U.S. and the world economy has increased." \par \par \uc2  In the immediate aftermath of the ECB cut, the euro jumped to around $0.9235, but fell back to around $0.9195, down from $0.9296 in London and from $0.9215 late Friday in New York. The dollar was at Y117.70, up from Y117.01 in London and Y117.22 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  But while the dollar did get some respite from the rate cuts and is well off its low of $0.9333 from earlier in the global session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term direction is being influenced more by the apparent resilience of U.S. stocks and looking further ahead is still set to run into a host of negatives. \par \par \uc2  U.S. equities did plunge after the opening Monday, the first day of trading since two planes slammed into the twin towers of the World Trade Center last Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But after being down 629 points, the Dow Jones Industrial Index appears to have stabilized and early afternoon in New York was down 531 points, or 5.5% at 9073 while the Nasdaq Composite Index, after being off 109 points, is down 79 points. \par \par \uc2  That partial recovery has led to a mirror recovery in the dollar and set the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tone for now. \par \par \uc2  "From here, the dollar's going to follow any upside for stocks pretty closely," said Barrow, at Bear Stearns. "It's trading stocks, tick for tick." \par \par \uc2  Fundamentally, and despite the global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts, many economists believe the stark economic facts point to further dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  For one, global investor confidence has taken a major hit, which is sure to directly affect foreign appetite for U.S. assets and in turn the U.S. ability to finance its current account deficit, currently estimated at around 4.5% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "We still think that the issue for the dollar is capital flows," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Sinche argues that last week's events greatly reduces the chances of a cyclical rebound in the dollar later this year. \par \par \uc2  "Consumer confidence is likely to suffer a further lurch downward and catastrophic events often leave consumers at home rather than in stores and restaurants, even when fundamentals are not deteriorating as they already have been," he said. \par \par \uc2  Even a $40-billion emergency fiscal package to help the U.S. recover from the attacks and retaliate against the individuals and governments responsible isn't likely to provide much in the way of broader stimulus to the economy, analysts reckon. \par \par \uc2  "Look at the America-wide hit to confidence, to travel, to tourism," said Barrow. "You only have to look at one of those areas and it outweighs anything you're going to see in New York." \par \par \uc2  At least half of the package must be used for recovery and assistance efforts and will go mostly to New York, Virginia and Pennsylvania, site of the crash of the fourth hijacked plane. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, many market watchers caution that it's still too early to begin to forecast trends for major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Not only is it not time to try and predict the markets, it's kind of in bad taste to predict the markets," said Laurie Cameron, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan private bank. \par \par \uc2  "Our focus is on keeping people calm, we're not initiating any new positions, we're not recommending any new positions." \par \par \uc2  The unprecedented situation in which the U.S. now finds itself has also made even the most confident of dollar forecasters uncertain about the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Usually the beneficiary of global economic upheaval, the U.S. now finds itself in the unusual situation of being in the eye of the hurricane and thus no longer the obvious global safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . U.S. assets, similarly, are no longer the world's most obvious and attractive investments. \par \par \uc2  It's nearly "impossible to conjecture" whether the dollar decline will be sustained, said C.Fred Bergsten, director of the Institute for International Economics in Washington, as the implications for the dollar in light of Tuesday's terrorist attacks can "cut both ways." \par \par \uc2  "There's a sense that this weakens the economy, but on the other hand, the [idea of the] traditional safe haven might move money into the dollar," Bergsten said. \par \par \uc2  Bergsten, a onetime Assistant Treasury Secretary for International Affairs, has been an outspoken proponent of a more realistic dollar policy and has argued there never was much substance to strong dollar pronouncements. \par \par \uc2  Prior to the attacks, Bergsten argued the Bush Administration should develop a "sound dollar" policy, under which it and its G7 partners would gradually ease the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down and thus avoid any sharp slides in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In comments to CNBC Monday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O' Neill reiterated the U.S. strong dollar policy and called the global round of interest rate cuts appropriate.` \par \par \uc2  For now, most analysts don't expect sharp slides, with many investors remaining firmly on the sidelines to see how stock markets fare over the next few days and how the U.S. retaliates for the attacks. \par \par \uc2  "A prolonged conflict would not be good for the dollar but there are a lot of intangibles," said Greg Salvaggio, vice president of trading at Tempus Consulting in Washington DC. \par \par \uc2  Salvaggio noted that many of his trading counterparts "took a lot of action last week which explains why the euro gained 2-3% and they are sitting back to see how (stocks) works out." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Last week, we saw people "removing a lot of risk for one to two\par weeks," said Salvaggio.\par     -Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381;\par     grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (John Hardy contributed to this article.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010917dx9h009q1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
