\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Steadies vs. Euro, Yen Supported by Global Rate Cuts \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 1,008 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2001\par 01:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  Normally, it would have been a watershed day for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : a rare coordinated round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest-rate cuts, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a 7.1% slide in U.S. shares. \par \par \uc2  Yet, perhaps the most telling aspect of trade in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday was just how little \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved, with the dollar ending the day only slightly weaker against the euro and somewhat stronger against the yen, a further sign that last week's terrorist attacks on the U.S. haven't sparked panic among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors. \par \par \uc2  Late in the New York day, the euro was at 92.41 cents, well off its intraday high of 93.33 cents, but above 92.15 cents late Friday in New York. The dollar was at 117.73 yen, up from 117.01 yen in London and from 117.22 yen late Friday. The pound was at $1.4657, down from $1.4725 in London and below Friday's $1.4715. The dollar was also at 1.6037 Swiss francs, down from 1.6220 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  In gaining against the yen and limiting its slide against the euro, the dollar took heart from the rate cuts, led by the Federal Reserve, and the reality that even though U.S. equities did plunge on Monday, many investors had expected the slide to be worse. The fact that the stock market, situated just blocks from the site of last week's attacks on the World Trade Center, was able to function at all, was in itself a relief to stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and a stool for the dollar. The rate cuts united most of the Group of Seven nations, with the Fed kicking off the U.S. day with a half-point rate cut, followed in quick suit by the Bank of Canada and later by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Swiss National Bank and Swedish Riksbank. The Bank of England was the most notable odd-man out in the global cuts, but many analysts expect the British bank to ease rates on Tuesday. The easings went some way toward achieving what many analysts believe was their primary goal: to inject a jolt of confidence into markets and consumers in the wake of last week's terrorist attacks. But given that each \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates a half point, the global easings don't alter the fundamental risks to global growth, nor the fundamental outlook for major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts reckon. \par \par \uc2  "The impact probably will not be the same as if in normal circumstances," said Jean-Francois Mercier, economist at Salomon Smith Barney in London. "This is more showing markets that there is coordination, that there is somebody at the helm and everybody on board agrees with the direction." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the fact that the central banks moved more or less the same way and by the same amount, obviously makes the impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  much less than if one bank had moved on its own. \par \par \uc2  "After this point, you've got to be broadly neutral on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Carl Weinberg, international economist at High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, N.Y., noting that growth prospects will be affected symmetrically across countries and that the interest rate differential between the U.S. and euro zone remains the same. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the global session, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  made good on its oft-repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threat, stepping into the market in Asian trade and buying dollars for yen in a bid to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While the Bank of Japan's move was long anticipated, the coordinated rate cuts were surprising to some market watchers, particularly as the ECB had left rates unchanged last week. What's more, ECB president Wim Duisenberg refuted suggestions earlier in the day that Monday morning's cut by the Fed should be taken as some sort of warning for the euro-zone's own economic prospects in the wake of the attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  This raises the question as to why the ECB didn't act last Thursday at its regular monetary policy meeting, or even why the Fed itself waited until Monday morning to cut rates. \par \par \uc2  The answer -- most economists speculate -- lies in the stock market. By waiting until Monday, the central banks around the world had a chance to try to limit the inevitable stock sell-off on the first day of U.S. trading since last Tuesday's attacks. \par \par \uc2  "I think that when history writes the book on this, what we'll find is the Fed didn't move last week in anticipation of a rocky market opening and wanted to keep some ammunition," said Mr. Weinberg. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the dollar's immediate outlook is going to be dictated by how equities fare for the rest of the week. \par \par \uc2  "From here, the dollar's going to follow any upside for stocks pretty closely," said Steve Barrow, analyst at Bear Stearns in London. "It's trading stocks, tick for tick." \par \par \uc2  Fundamentally, and despite the global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts, many economists believe the stark economic facts point to further dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  For one, global investor confidence has taken a major hit, which is sure to directly affect foreign appetite for U.S. assets and in turn the U.S. ability to finance its current account deficit, currently estimated at around 4.5% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  "We still think that the issue for the dollar is capital flows," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Sinche argues that last week's events greatly reduce the chances of a cyclical rebound in the dollar later this year. \par \par \uc2  "Consumer confidence is likely to suffer a further lurch downward and catastrophic events often leave consumers at home rather than in stores and restaurants, even when fundamentals are not deteriorating as they already have been," he said. \par \par \uc2  Write to Grainne McCarthy at grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020010917dx9h01oht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
