\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  To Weaken Yen After US Woes Hurt Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 12:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday in an effort to curb the yen's rise, a move reflecting concerns that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could pummel the struggling economy by strangling exports and exacerbating deflation. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said in a statement that authorities took "appropriate action" - a code phrase for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when used in the context of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to closely monitor the market and take appropriate action, as necessary, for the stability of exchange rates," Shiokawa added. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came after the dollar had plunged more than three yen since Friday to Y116.83 Monday morning, its lowest level in nearly seven months. The dollar jumped more than a yen and a half after the action but later surrendered most of those gains. \par \par \uc2  Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities reflected heightened fears about conditions in financial markets in the wake of the terrorist attacks in the U.S. last Tuesday. Ever since that cataclysmic event, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been in a tailspin and many stock markets around the world have fallen sharply. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock markets are scheduled to open Monday for the first time since the attacks, ending the longest period of closure since World War I. \par \par \uc2  Turbulence in financial markets stems from concerns that the shock of the attacks could chill U.S. consumer sentiment and throw the world's largest economy into recession. \par \par \uc2  In Japan, for example, the fallout from an exacerbated slowdown in the U.S. could put extreme pressure on the economy, which is already in a steep downturn and likely headed for recession. A strong yen would undermine exports, which are already falling fast because of slack demand overseas, and deal a blow to Japan's struggling manufacturers. \par \par \uc2  An appreciation in the yen also puts heavy downward pressure on prices and could complicate the Bank of Japan's efforts to stamp out deflation, which is ravaging corporate balance sheets and inflating the costs of the debt maintenance when measured in real terms. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's policy board is scheduled to gather Tuesday for a two-day meeting. Many analysts expect the bank to loosen monetary policy in a bid to curb the yen's rise and help stabilize stocks prices. The Nikkei 225 stock average dropped 504.48 points, or 5.0%, Monday to 9504.41 - the lowest close since December, 1983. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said Japanese monetary officials remain in close contact with U.S. counterparts, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday was conducted alone. A key question going forward will be how monetary officials in Europe and the U.S. respond to the action. Although few traders expect other central banks to join the effort to boost the dollar, comments welcoming the BOJ's dollar buying might have a major psychological impact. \par \par \uc2  Market participants estimate the Bank of Japan sold around $1 billion worth of yen against the dollar and perhaps the euro in a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - a token amount that represented a warning shot across the bows of the ultra-thin market rather than a determined effort to drive the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  "I hope the market understands that our stance is unchanged," said Zembei Mizoguchi, the head of the ministry's international bureau. \par \par \uc2  Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley, said Japanese authorities will continue to put a floor under the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at stabilizing the dollar/yen for now," Umemoto said. "If the (U.S.) financial markets recover, we may see more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift the dollar above Y120." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par arran.scott@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1135 4201 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dx9h02iu7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
