\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 - \highlight22\uc2 Central bank \highlight\uc2  reassurance lifts crisis-hit dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 488 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2001\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Wednesday, clawing back almost half the previous day's hefty losses, after central banks said they were ready to act to dampen market volatility. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Swiss National Bank and the Bank of Japan all moved to add liquidity to the financial system, while ECB President Wim Duisenberg said the bank was prepared to offer any help when needed, or if asked to by the United States. \par \par \uc2  Panic-selling in the wake of Tuesday's unprecedented terror attacks in New York and Washington had sent the dollar tumbling as much as three percent against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But dealers said investors were now keen to unwind short dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "Central banks have moved to reassure markets over liquidity and have hinted at further action if need be," said Mark Henry, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at GNI in London. \par \par \uc2  "This reassurance has lifted the dollar, as has the realisation that the terrorist attacks are no longer a U.S. problem but a global one." \par \par \uc2  News that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan would return to the United States from Switzerland on Wednesday fanned speculation that major industrialised countries could coordinate a round of interest rate cuts to ease fears of a global recession. \par \par \uc2  Such talk gained ground after Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders said Group of Seven countries may issue a statement later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Talk of possible action to stabilise the dollar was also fuelled after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be necessary if \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were volatile. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR BOUNCES BACK \par \par \uc2  The dollar leapt more than one percent to highs around $0.9015 to the euro and more than 1.5 percent against the Swiss franc to peaks above 1.67. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also rebounded half a percent to 119.70 yen, clawing back from losses of as much as three yen on Tuesday. But it eased off its highs by 1145 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing some unwinding of yesterday's knee-jerk reaction to U.S. attacks," said Steven Pearson, treasury economist at Halifax in London. "But trading is volatile and price movements are exaggerated." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said much of the market was still paralysed in the aftermath of the destruction of the World Trade Center, home to many financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  Many banks in Europe refrained from interbank dealing as a mark of respect for the tragedy and there was uncertainty over whether New York banks would be open at all. \par \par \uc2  There was also concern as to whether \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements could be completed smoothly. \par \par \uc2  While no specific problems were reported with clearing systems, the Bank of England said the Federal Reserve had asked all central banks to minimise trading in dollars to avoid exacerbating financial market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  The international financial markets association, the ACI, also called on banks to refrain from unnecessary trading activity. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx9c016r2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
