\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Japan shudders, Nikkei plunges after US tragedy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2001\par 12:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Japan shuddered on Wednesday at the vivid televised images of the vicious aircraft attacks on landmark U.S. buildings, with intense fears over the global economic implications overshadowed only by horror and disbelief. \par \par \uc2  The sight of the World Trade Center, a symbol of U.S. economic might, imploding in a plume of dust and smoke left investors aghast and sent them another signal to sell Japanese stocks, pushing the Nikkei average below psychological support of 10,000 for the first time in 17 years. \par \par \uc2  Government officials called for a stiff upper lip but to no avail, given Japan's economy is already teetering on the edge of its fourth recession in a decade and the fallout from Tuesday's horrific news could fuel the downturn in world growth. \par \par \uc2  "The overseas shock has hit us when sentiment is already bad," Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Over the past week, Japan has had bad economic news on a daily basis. \par \par \uc2  Last Thursday, credit rating agency Moody's Investors Service warned it might downgrade its rating on Japan's government bonds. On Friday that was followed by data showing a 0.8 percent contraction in gross domestic product for the April-June quarter. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, data showed machinery orders, a leading indicator for capital investment, dropping for a third straight month in July, and Moody's rival, Standard & Poor's, gave Japan another ratings warning. \par \par \uc2  "The deflationary impact of what's taking place already, plus what happened yesterday, puts Japan teetering on the edge of a deflationary spiral," said Mineko Sasaki-Smith, chief strategist at PricewaterhouseCoopers in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  REFORM POLICY UNDER PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  The dismal economic news added to the dilemma of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, criticised by those who think the economy will struggle because his reforms are too slow and by those who think it will suffer because they are too aggressive. \par \par \uc2  His plans to accelerate banks' bad-loan writeoffs and fiscal discipline are seen increasing bankruptcies and exacerbating a jobless rate already running at a record 5.0 percent in July. \par \par \uc2  Takenaka said he saw no need for knee-jerk reactions in monetary or fiscal policy. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think there is a need immediately to change the direction of monetary policy or other economic measures in a drastic way," Takenaka told a news conference. "But we will continue to monitor the situation carefully." \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the Nikkei stock average ended more than six percent lower, roughly matching losses European markets suffered after the U.S. news broke on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  It closed down 682.85 points or 6.63 percent at 9,610.10, its lowest close since December 1983 and its eighth-largest percentage decline ever. \par \par \uc2  Financial Services Minister Hakuo Yanagisawa, the guardian of Japan's financial system, urged market participants to stay calm and Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would provide ample liquidity in the money market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved quickly by injecting extra funds into the banking system early on Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  But the yield on benchmark 10-year Japanese government bonds edged up to 4-= month highs of 1.450 percent, reflecting concerns that the deteriorating economy would force Koizumi to abandon fiscal discipline and issue more bonds to finance a big fiscal stimulus package. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese government at this stage has not ruled out any of their policy options," said Graeme Knowd, senior financial analyst at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said short-term fiscal measures have failed to kick-start Japan's economy and Koizumi's credibility to push ahead with reform may be at stake. \par \par \uc2  "Certainly this will give (Koizumi) leeway (on policies). But he will be judged on policies over the next few months to a year rather than spur-of-the-moment pressures... so I am not ready to say he will turn around," said Sasaki-Smith. \par \par \uc2  On \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where a sharp yen rise would hurt exporters' profits, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa did not rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "If the unstable conditions persist, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be necessary," Shiokawa said. "We must prevent speculative forces from becoming too strong." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa spoke to visiting U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill by telephone and he said they agreed to a close exchange of information, but added that they had not discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  O'Neill cut short his trip to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday and headed back to the United States to help to settle markets in the wake of the attacks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was hovering above 119 yen in late Tokyo trade after diving more than three yen to 118.50 yen when the news broke on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx9c012w8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
