\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dlr regains footing in wake of attacks on U.S. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2001\par 09:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The dollar regained its footing against the euro in choppy trade on Wednesday, recovering about a quarter of two percent losses sustained in the wake of the previous day's devastating attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar was still struggling against the yen as hefty losses in Japanese stocks, which fell to a fresh 17-year low, fuelled worries Japanese firms would need to bring home foreign funds to bolster balance sheets ahead of end-September book closing. \par \par \uc2  However, trading volumes were very thin and dealers said the market was paralysed in the aftermath of the destruction of the World Trade Center, home to many financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  "Liquidity in the market is very thin today and activity will be very low," said Hans Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. \par \par \uc2  "People were already looking for euro/dollar to go higher and we should see only limited gains." \par \par \uc2  Traders said they were also pondering the likely after-effects of Tuesday's blasts that also severly damaged the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  At 0726 GMT, the euro was trading just below $0.91 in choppy trading which had earlier seen the euro rise within sight of Tuesday's 11-day high around $0.9170. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood around 119 yen, having fallen from highs above 122 yen on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was firmer around 1.6620 Swiss francs, up over one percent on the day, falling to seven-lows a day ago. \par \par \uc2  MARKET UNERTAINTY \par \par \uc2  Most traders said they were still struggling to grasp the implications of the unprecedented attack. \par \par \uc2  There were also concerns over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlements can be completed smoothly. \par \par \uc2  Central banks around the world said they would act to provide further liquidity into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank said it was ready to provide extra liquidity if it was demanded by banks. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, meanwhile, said he was considering \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If the unstable conditions persist, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be necessary," Shiokawa told a news conference. "We must prevent speculative forces from becoming too strong." \par \par \uc2  He said he did not talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he spoke with U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill on the phone on Wednesday. A planned Tokyo meeting between the two on Wednesday was called off when O'Neill cut short a visit to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to return home. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be difficult in current illiquid trading conditions. \par \par \uc2  The yen gained some three percent against the dollar on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Even if you want to pay, you can't do it without an office," said Akio Makabe, chief economist at Dai-ichi Kangyo Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  "It seems difficult to send an e-mail from New York today. It will take some time before \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  get back to normal in New York," he added. \par \par \uc2  The market was also awaiting a speech by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg to the European Parliament. \par \par \uc2  CONSUMER CONFIDENCE \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the attacks could hit already-shaky consumer confidence in the U.S. and the dollar would suffer further. \par \par \uc2  "When no one really know what's happening next to the U.S., there will likely be a flight of capital out of the dollar," said Hajime Kawamura, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Corp. \par \par \uc2  "Money is cowardly. When something like this happens, foreign investors will repatriate funds out of the U.S," he said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts also felt the dollar's fall would probably be limited by the fact that the effects of the attack would be felt around the world. \par \par \uc2  "The initial response was obviously to sell the dollar. But given the current fragile global economy, the impact will not be limited to America," said Taisuke Tanaka, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  He also said that emergency economic measures such as a cut in U.S. interest rates would probably be introduced to provide support for the U.S. economy, which should halt the dollar slide. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx9c00z15\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
