\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Japan Promises To Support Econ, But Markets Buckle \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2001\par 10:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged Wednesday to do everything possible to shore up the economy and stabilize financial markets after the worst terrorist attack in U.S. history sent global stock markets into a tailspin and the dollar plunging. \par \par \uc2  The prospects for Japan's ailing economy, already in a sharp downturn and hamstrung by a debt-clogged financial system, grew even bleaker as Tokyo stock prices plummeted to their lowest level in nearly 18 years and the yen soared, threatening to choke off exports and stifle the corporate sector. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi called the attacks on New York and Washington "extremely cowardly and unforgivable" and said it's essential to prevent the tragedy from disrupting the Japanese and world economy. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said that "temporarily, there will be big shocks" to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy said that the ministry is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, with other Group of Seven industrial powers if needed, to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take "all necessary measures" to ensure smooth fund settlements and stability in the financial markets. The BOJ flooded the banking system with Y8.3 trillion, well above its already generous Y6 trillion target. \par \par \uc2  The tough talk and aggressive monetary measures may have helped ward off a meltdown, but they didn't revive sentiment among market participants, who remained stunned a day after the suicide attacks on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei 225 Stock Average shed 6.6%, or 682.85 points, to close at 9610.10, its lowest close since December, 1983. The Nikkei's eighth-worst percentage drop likely would have been worse - German shares sank 8.5% Tuesday - if the authorities hadn't imposed circuit breakers on market declines. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which crumbled more than Y3 in the wake of the attacks, touched a low of Y118.73 in very thin trading, lower than the Y118.89 marked late Tuesday U.S. East Coast time. Players virtually ignored Shiokawa's warning of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the G7. \par \par \uc2  Japanese government bond prices were mixed, with longer-maturity issues rising in price on a flight to quality but shorter-term bonds falling on concerns that Koizumi will have to ramp up government spending to deal with the economic mess. \par \par \uc2  The forceful response from authorities to the fallout from the U.S. events underscores the high stakes for Japan. Having struggled in vain for more than a decade to achieve a durable recovery after the nation's asset bubble collapsed, Japanese authorities have all but emptied their war chest of policy tools. \par \par \uc2  With government debt at dangerously high levels, fiscal policy options are limited. The BOJ has pushed interest rates down near zero by pumping massive amounts of money into the economy, but deflation still continues to deepen. \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's underscored the policy bind Tuesday when it cut Japan's sovereign rating outlook to negative from stable. Highlighting the ravages of deflation, second quarter nominal gross domestic product imploded at a 10.3% annualized pace in the second quarter from the January-March period. \par \par \uc2  Already staggering, Japan is now vulnerable to damage to the U.S. economy - a key export market - higher oil prices, weaker asset prices and the strong yen, said economist Richard Jerram at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  "The impact is unlikely to be large, but Japan is already so weak, it is in a poor position to bear additional bad news," Jerram said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010912dx9c000l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
