\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED -  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen hurt by Japan gloom, market awaits payrolls. \b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2001\par 13:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (In the LONDON item "Yen hurt by Japan gloom, market awaits payrolls", please read in the seventh paragraph "...just below three-week highs", correcting from "above three-week lows". \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows.) \par \par \uc2  (Updates price action, changes byline) \par \par \uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The yen fell more than a third of a percent agains t the dollar early on Friday, hurt by growing pessimism over the Japanese economy, but as the session moved on the market remained cautious ahead of key U.S. jobs data. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro eased a touch against the dollar after a sharp rebound from four-week lows the previous day, weighed by a larger-than-expected fall in German manufacturing output. \par \par \uc2  Data on Friday showed Japan's gross domestic product fell 0.8 percent in the second quarter. The figure was slightly better than analysts' forecasts of a 0.9 percent drop but not enough to allay fears Japan is slipping into its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  "The data showed what we know that Japan is weak but it may be even weaker in the third quarter," said Hans Redeker, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stratgy at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "But it is relatively quiet ahead of payrolls. If the number is in line with expectations or worse, it could boost the euro as the non-manufacturing NAPM (National Association of Purchasing Management) figures on Thursday already raised worries about the consumer sector." \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters expected U.S. non-farm payroll positions to have shrunk by 33,000 in August after declining 42,000 in July. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 121.30 yen at 1030 GMT, just blow recent three- week highs (correct). The euro tested one-week highs against the yen above 108.44 \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased slightly to $0.8945 . It had recovered from hefty losses earlier this week after the non-manufacturing NAPM survey released on Thursday undercut budding optimism of a rebound in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  NO REPRIEVE FOR YEN \par \par \uc2  News of the contraction in Japan's economy, although expected, was hardly good news for Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi as he embarks on an ambitious programme of reforms. \par \par \uc2  Within an hour of the release of the data, Koizumi gave his cabinet the go-ah ead to prepare an extra budget to try to boost the economy and deal with unemployment. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there is now a possibility that the government's promise to cap government debt issuance under 30 trillion yen won't be kept and this is hurting yen sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which has already lost as much as two percent against the dollar this week, will likely take its cues from any comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Tokyo and Washington policymakers this weekend. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa and U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill are scheduled to attend a meeting of finance ministers at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum, which is being held in China until September 9. \par \par \uc2  They will meet again in Tokyo when O'Neill visits Japan next week.But analyst s said they did not expect the U.S. would join Japan in taking overt steps to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  "It would be bizarre and really antagonistic to U.S. manufacturers if the U.S. authorities took steps to help Japanese exporters while ignoring their own exporters," said Ryan Shea, international economist at BankOne. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose some five percent against the dollar in August but has already shed two percent this week, hurt by ongoing concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy and fears of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem its rise. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dx9702bsx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
