\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro feels the heat as recent rally stalls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 813 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2001\par 14:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The euro fell to three-week lows against the dollar on Tuesday and plumbed its weakest level for six weeks against the yen as its faltering rally encouraged investors to get out of long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. computer giant Hewlett-Packard was buying Compaq Computer Corp in a move deemed positive for U.S. stocks had helped spur dollar buying but analysts said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was able to hold off further pressure as attention turned to U.S. manufacturing data due later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's one percent gain against the euro was matched by a similar gain on the Swiss franc and a half percent rise on the yen but analysts said this was a shakeout of long euro positions rather than a turnaround in the dollar's fortunes. \par \par \uc2  "I'd advise against extrapolating the movements of the last three to four sessions into the start of a new medium term trend," said Nick Parsons, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro has been caught in a range of $0.90-$0.92 for the last three weeks and its repeated failure to break above $0.92 provided a cue for investors to take profits, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Hewlett-Packard's planned $25 billion purchase of Compaq was deemed positive for the U.S. stock market and had been hailed by some in European share markets as potentially heralding further consolidation activity. \par \par \uc2  But Parsons said the dollar's failure to push ahead was due in part to market second thoughts that the deal was defensive and likely to lead to job cuts in a tough environment. \par \par \uc2  "It's a merger about job cutting and it's symptomatic of the problems the U.S. economy is going to face over the next quarter," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was defending levels close to the day's low of $0.8966 , at $.8975 by 1155 GMT. It is now nearly three percent percent below 5-1/2 month peaks scaled last month. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro set six-week troughs around 106.85 before trimming losses to around 107.10. Analysts said there was also unwinding of long euro/yen positions. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also scaled three-week highs around 1.6905 Swiss francs . \par \par \uc2  FOCUS SWINGS TO U.S. \par \par \uc2  Attention was now turning to the U.S. National Association of Purchasing Managers' (NAPM) survey for August due at 1400 GMT whose main index is forecast to rise to 43.9 after 43.6 in July. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen a good rebound in the Chicago NAPM index (on Friday) which has in the past been a good lead," said Hans Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "So we might have a chance of seeing the national index above 44 - that could provide further support to the U.S. equity market," he added. \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. returning to business after Monday's Labor Day holiday, one trader said the market there could view the euro as too low and take it higher initially. "But I think the sentiment will be: watch the stock markets for the bigger flows," he said. \par \par \uc2  Further out, key August U.S. jobs data due on Friday is likely to provide the main spotlight, after recent concerns about the pace and strength of economic recovery there. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN'S MIXED YEN TALK \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose half a percent against the yen, after Japanese officials reiterated they would act to cap the yen's recent export-crimping strength. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, including top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda, said they thought authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if irregular moves were seen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  But reported remarks by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami caused some confusion. He was first quoted as saying he was not thinking of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in any case up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Hayami caught everyone on the hop - he said they weren't looking to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market sold it, then he contradicted himself and caught the market short. The market took it he was saying something dramatic and he wasn't," a trader here said. \par \par \uc2  The net effect was a half percent dollar gain to 119.40 yen . \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless the verbal threats from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have put the market on alert for official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, especially since Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Sunday he would ask what the U.S. government's position is on the dollar when he meets U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill later this week. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill and Shiokawa meet at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) conference in China on September 6-9 and again in Tokyo around September 12 when O'Neill visits Japan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010905dx9400ai4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
