\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro under pressure as recent rally falters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2001\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The euro fell to three-week lows against the dollar and six-week troughs against the yen on Tuesday, as frustration at the euro's inability to extend recent gains encouraged investors to unwind long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. computer giant Hewlett-Packard has agreed to buy Compaq Computer Corp boosted the outlook for U.S. stocks, helping the dollar rally one percent against the euro. It also rose half a percent against the yen as comments from Japanese officials kept the market on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alert. \par \par \uc2  The euro has been caught in a range of $0.90-$0.92 for the last three weeks and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated failure to break above $0.92 had encouraged investors to take profits. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing some unwinding of long euro positions after the euro's strong rally," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "There is no direct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  implication from the Hewlett-Packard/Compaq deal but it is having a positive impact on equities and may provide a catalyst for profit taking on the euro." \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to three-week lows around $0.8966 , shedding one percent on the day. It is now nearly three percent percent below 5-1/2 month peaks scaled last month. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro set six-week troughs around 106.85 before trimming losses to around 107.10 by 1025 GMT. Analysts said there was also unwinding of long euro/yen positions. \par \par \uc2  Hewlett-Packard is buying Compaq for some $25 billion in stock, news that has lifted U.S. stock futures. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS SWINGS TO U.S. \par \par \uc2  Attention was now turning to the U.S. National Association of Purchasing Managers' (NAPM) survey for August due at 1400 GMT whose main index is forecast to rise to 43.9 after 43.6 in July. \par \par \uc2  "We've seen a good rebound in the Chicago NAPM index (on Friday) which has in the past been a good lead," said Hans Redeker, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "So we might have a chance of seeing the national index above 44 - that could provide further support to the U.S. equity market," he added. \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. returning to business after Monday's Labor Day holiday, one trader said the market there could view the euro as too low and take it higher intially. "But I think the sentiment will be: watch the stock markets for the bigger flows," he said. \par \par \uc2  Further out, key August U.S. jobs data due on Friday is likely to provide the main spotlight, after recent concerns about the pace and strength of economic recovery there. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN'S MIXED YEN TALK \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose half a percent against the yen, after Japanese officials reiterated they would act to cap the yen's recent export-crimping strength. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials, including top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda, said they thought authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if irregular moves were seen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  But reported remarks made by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami caused some confusion. He was first quoted as saying he was not thinking of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in any case up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Hayami caught everyone on the hop - he said they weren't looking to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market sold it, then he contradicted himself and caught the market short. The market took it he was saying something dramatic and he wasn't," a trader here said. \par \par \uc2  The net effect was a half percent dollar gain to 119.30 yen . \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless the verbal threats from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have put the market on alert to official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, especially since Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Sunday he would ask what the U.S. government's position is on the dollar when he meets U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill later this week. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill and Shiokawa meet at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) conference in China on September 6-9 and again in Tokyo around September 12 when O'Neill visits Japan. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010905dx9400adl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
