\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro on defensive as recent rally disappoints. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2001\par 09:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Adds Bank of Japan comments, changes byline and dateline) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The euro fell to three-week lows against the dollar and six-week troughs against the yen on Tuesday, as frustration at the euro's inability to extend recent gains encouraged investors to unwind long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  News that U.S. Hewlett-Packard has agreed to buy Compaq Computer Corp boosted the outlook for U.S. stocks, bolstering the dollar. But the dollar was pulled in two directions against the yen following contradictory comments from Japanese officials on action to cap the yen's export-crimping strength. \par \par \uc2  The euro has been caught in a range of around $0.90-$0.92 for the last three weeks and traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated failure to break above $0.92 had encouraged investors to take profits. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing some unwinding of long euro positions after the euro's strong rally," said Neil MacKinnon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  "There is no direct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  implication from the Hewlett-Packard/Compaq deal but it is having a positive impact on equities and may provide a catalyst for profit taking on the euro." \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to three-week lows around $0.8990, down three-quarters of a percent on the day. It is now over 2-1/2 percent below 5-1/2 month peaks scaled last month. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro set six-week troughs around 106.85 before trimming losses to around 107.10 by 0739 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Hewlett-Packard is buying Compaq for some $25 billion in stock, news that has lifted U.S. stock futures. Traders said worries over the lagged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact of Deutsche Telekom's purchase of U.S. operator VoiceStream helped undermine the euro. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN'S MIXED YEN TALK \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was caught in a tug of war against the dollar after conflicting comments on recent yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami was quoted as saying by the Jiji news agency that he is not thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hayami added he has not heard there will be talks to stem the yen's rise at the next meeting of Group of Seven industrialised nations. \par \par \uc2  This followed earlier remarks by Takahiro Mitani, manager of the Bank of Japan's Osaka Branch in western \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that he thought \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if irregular moves were seen on the foreign exchanges. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, meanwhile, reiterated recent yen strength was abnormal and that the authorities would take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the situation warranted. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the verbal threats from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had put the market on alert to official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, especially since Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Sunday he would ask what the U.S. government's position is on the dollar when he meets U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill later this week. \par \par \uc2  O'Neill and Shiokawa are attending a meeting of the finance ministers of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) in China from September 6 to 9, and they meet again in Tokyo around September 12 during O'Neill's visit to Japan. \par \par \uc2  "There are massive positions in euro/yen being held and we could see a bit of a clearout," said a trader at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was hovering around 118.95 yen, up slightly on the day. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DATA WATCH \par \par \uc2  Market attention was seen turning to the National Association of Purchasing Management (NAPM) August survey at 1400 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Economists surveyed by Reuters expected the NAPM index to rise to 43.9 in August from 43.6 in July. \par \par \uc2  But given recent concerns about the pace and strength of economic recovery in the United States, key August U.S. jobs data due on Friday was expected to fall under the spotlight. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was also some focus on reports that the Federal Reserve is prepared to continue cutting rates, a quarter point at a time, until signs of recovery emerge. \par \par \uc2  The Wall Street Journal reported in its online edition on Tuesday that there were indications from top Fed policy makers at a conference of the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City recently that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was prepared to continue cutting interest rates a quarter point at a time. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010904dx9400gda\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
