\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen unfazed by  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  threats, euro wilts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Christina Fincher \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2001\par 16:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The yen was steady within half a percent of recent three-month peaks against the dollar on Monday as further threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were offset by repatriation flows ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's end-September book closing. \par \par \uc2  The euro, meanwhile, shed half a percent against the dollar and the yen, as a weakening chart outlook and worries over the lagged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact of Deutsche Telekom's purchase of U.S. operator VoiceStream encouraged dealers to exit long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  However, the yen took centre stage as Japan stepped up its bid to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-crimping strength. Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto said Japan would take appropriate action if rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were seen, while confirmation his boss will meet the U.S. Treasury Secretary for talks on September 12 helped keep markets on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alert. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is the main focus," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. "Repatriation flows are nearing an end and there is a risk that rhetoric from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  could turn into outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen continues to strengthen." \par \par \uc2  Under new accounting rules, Japanese companies must declare the market value of their investments at the fiscal half-year end. Monday's slide in Japanese stocks to a fresh 17-year low only added to pressure to bring home foreign funds for balance sheet purposes. \par \par \uc2  At 1405 GMT, the yen was trading around 118.85 per dollar, little changed from Friday's New York close. It stood around 107.80 per euro, within sight of Friday's three-week highs. Trading volumes were thin due to the U.S. Labor Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN STEPS UP VERBAL CAMPAIGN \par \par \uc2  Muto's comments came after Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa reiterated on Sunday the government was in contact with its Group of Seven partners about recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. \par \par \uc2  The yen has gained about five percent against the dollar in the past month, fuelling concern that Japan's exports will be stifled at a time when the economy is already flirting with recession. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa officially talks to U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill in Tokyo next week but the two could meet later this week at a meeting of finance ministers of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in China. \par \par \uc2  "With the Japanese authorities becoming more aggressive in their stance, the suggestion is that we are closer to actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "As we move closer to next week's meeting, there is a risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but this is likely to be unilateral at this stage." \par \par \uc2  The weakness of Japan's economy is expected to be highlighted by Friday's growth data which is expected to show the economy contracted by around 0.8 percent in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome, coupled with the persistent slide in Japanese stocks would pile pressure on the Bank of Japan to ease yet again when it meets later this month, said analysts. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar could face pressure of its own with the U.S. National Association of Purchasing Management survey and jobs data for August due for release later this week. \par \par \uc2  EURO UNDER PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  The euro fell half a percent to day's lows around $0.9065, down almost two cents from 5-1/2 month highs set on August 22. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro's inability to regain a foothold above $0.92 last week had dented its chart outlook, but added that buy orders at $0.9050 meant the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being supported within its recent range. \par \par \uc2  "The euro failure to regain $0.92 on Friday has spilled into negative sentiment in thin trading today," said a trader at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "The market is very long euros and people are looking for excuses to unwind these positions." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were also some concerns that a flowback of shares from Deutsche Telekom's acquisition of U.S. company VoiceStream could spur selling of billions of euros against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly dollars. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Telekom completed its purchase of VoiceStream in May. But former shareholders in the U.S. firm agreed a lock-up provision that prevented them from immediately dumping their newly acquired shares in the German telecoms group. \par \par \uc2  A major lock-up period relating to the acquisition expired at the weekend, meaning some 200 million Telekom shares could potentially flow back on to the market. Telekom shares were around 16.58 euros each in afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010903dx9300sej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
