\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen in spotlight on Japan jawboning, euro soft. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 792 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2001\par 12:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The yen was steady within half a percent of recent three-month peaks against the dollar on Monday as verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was countered by repatriation flows from Japanese investors before end-September book closing. \par \par \uc2  The euro was the worst performer, falling about half a percent against the dollar and the yen, hurt by Friday's encouraging U.S. manufacturing reports and concern over the lagged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact of Deutsche Telekom's recent purchase of U.S. VoiceStream. \par \par \uc2  Japan stepped up efforts to stem the yen's export-denting strength. Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto said Japan would take appropriate action if rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were seen, while his boss Masajuro Shiokawa on Sunday reiterated Japan was in contact with its Group of Seven partners about recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gyrations. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is the main focus," said Steven Saywell at Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  "Repatriation flows are nearing an end and there is a risk that rhetoric from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  could turn into outright \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen continues to strengthen." \par \par \uc2  Under new accounting rules, Japanese companies and banks will be forced to declare the market value of their investments at the fiscal half-year end. Sliding Japanese stocks, which closed at a fresh 17-year low on Monday, have added to pressure to bring home foreign funds for balance sheet purposes. \par \par \uc2  At 0957 GMT, the yen was trading around 118.80 per dollar, little changed on the day but within half a percent of Friday's three-month peaks. It stood around 107.90 per euro, within sight of Friday's three-week highs. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading volumes were thin due to the U.S. Labor Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  YEN UNDER SCRUTINY \par \par \uc2  Muto's comments kept markets on edge over yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Shiokawa on Sunday reiterated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in contact with its Group of Seven partners about recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. \par \par \uc2  He said he would inquire into the U.S. government's position on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he meets U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill later this week. \par \par \uc2  The two will attend a meeting of finance ministers of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in China from September 6 to 9, and they meet again in Tokyo around September 12 during O'Neill's visit to Japan. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese authorities have made it clear they don't want the yen to appreciate further," said Nick Stamenkovic, senior strategist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  "However, the yen is holding firm for now, largely because of expectations of repatriation flows before the fiscal half-year end. \par \par \uc2  Officials at the Federal Reserve and the Treasury on Friday declined to confirm whether there had been any G7 discussions about \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while a spokesman at the German finance ministry said it had not been contacted on the matter. \par \par \uc2  SOFTER EURO \par \par \uc2  The euro softened to around $0.9075, more than one percent below Friday's one-week highs, undermined by a couple of U.S. manufacturing indicators on Friday which beat expectations and stirred hopes recession in industry might be stabilising. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was also some concern that a flowback of shares from Deutsche Telekom's acquisition of U.S. VoiceStream could spur sales of billions of euros against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly dollars. \par \par \uc2  Deutsche Telekom completed its purchase of VoiceStream in May. But former shareholders in the U.S. firm agreed a lock-up provision that prevented them from immediately dumping their newly acquired Telekom shares. \par \par \uc2  A major lock-up period relating to the acquisition expired at the weekend, meaning some 200 million Telekom shares could potentially flow back on to the market. Telekom shares were around 16.15 euros each in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Such worries encouraged investors to unwind their long euro positions with little fresh direction expected due to the U.S. holiday. \par \par \uc2  Attention was also seen turning to this week's batch of economic news. \par \par \uc2  The yen could face trouble from Japanese growth data for the second quarter, due on Friday. Median forecasts are for a fall of around 0.8 percent, or 3.3 percent annualised. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome, coupled with the persistent slide in Japanese stocks would pile pressure on the Bank of Japan to ease yet again when it meets later this month, said analysts. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could face a test of its own, with the U.S. National Association of Purchasing Management survey and the payrolls report for August due for release later this week. \par \par \uc2  "The market now wants to see more evidence of what's happpening to the U.S. economy," said Citibank's Saywell. \par \par \uc2  German jobless data are also due this week along with figures on manufacturing orders and industrial output. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010903dx9300kj6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscale