\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar punished by concerns over U.S. economy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2001\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 31 (Reuters) - The dollar hit three-month lows against the yen and one-week lows against the euro on Friday as concern about the health of the U.S. economy mounted after the previous day's slide on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  The yen was the biggest gainer of the dollar's woes despite equities in Japan also suffering and data there painting an even gloomier economic picture, as increasingly risk-averse Japanese investors repatriated assets ahead of September book-closing. \par \par \uc2  The yen's half-percent gains against both the dollar and the euro prompted a raft of warnings from the Japanese authorities that they might act to stem further strength, with the finance minister even raising the spectre of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other Group of Seven countries. \par \par \uc2  "It's really a dollar issue. We're seeing it weaken, following the sell-off in equity markets and increasing concern the U.S. consumer will cut back," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at the Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "It's actually been one of the worst weeks in Japan in terms of equities and data. What's happening in the run-up to September is that Japanese investors are unwilling to take any risk." \par \par \uc2  At 1130 GMT, the dollar was trading around 118.65, more than half a percent lower than New York's closing levels. Meanwhile, the euro pushed against resistance at $0.92, within a whisker of its highest level in more than five months, before it dropped back down to around $0.9165. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREATENS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Gloomy corporate outlooks from key U.S. tech firms sent the blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average below the key 10,000 mark for the first time since April on Thursday, with the Nasdaq plumbing levels below 1,800. \par \par \uc2  The resulting dollar-selling momentum was so strong it prompted a blunt warning from Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that further yen strength could be met with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in contact with other G7 countries. \par \par \uc2  The German finance ministry, however, said that it had not had any "recent contact" with Japanese counterparts on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. \par \par \uc2  Still, analysts noted that history showed that Japan was always prepared to back up rhetoric on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with action, and would surely act even if it found little support from other G7 colleagues. \par \par \uc2  "What we are seeing is general dollar weakness and my view is the dollar is going to see a big correction," said Gerald Gardner, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  "But you can never rule out the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that further sudden upward moves in the yen would probably get the Japanese authorities putting their money where their mouths were. \par \par \uc2  "In our view, this year's low at around 118.30 yen is a line in the sand as far as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  goes," said Claudio Piron, global markets economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  FRANCE JOBLESS RISES \par \par \uc2  The euro was still benefiting from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  delivering a growth-boosting quarter-point interest rate cut on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts were concerned that it had not broken above the $0.92 level despite the weakness of U.S. stock markets. \par \par \uc2  They noted that the higher than expected rise in July's French jobless total showed the euro zone economy was also suffering. \par \par \uc2  Still, Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet struck an upbeat note saying he would not be surprised if growth this year turned out at 2.3 percent. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the market was also reluctant to take new positions ahead of Fed Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan speaking and the release of the Chicago PMI at 1400 GMT and the long weekend in the U.S. U.S. markets are closed for Labour Day on Monday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010831dx8v00j50\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
