\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Major  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  stand still, dealers await ECB. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mariko Hayashibara \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2001\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 30 (Reuters) - Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were trapped in a narrow band on Thursday as dealers held their breath ahead of the results of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key policy-setting meeting later in the day. \par \par \uc2  There are expectations for the ECB to cut its key refinancing rate by 25 basis points to 4.25 percent, but doubts linger after strong European M3 money supply data, which the ECB sees as a harbinger of future inflation. \par \par \uc2  "I can't predict whatsoever about the ECB's move," said Ryohei Muramatsu, manager of treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  The euro barely moved against the dollar, standing at $0.9080/85 against its late U.S. level of $0.9090. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro eased slightly and stood at 108.85/95 against 109.17 in late New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said yen-buying by Japanese exporters in dollar/yen indirectly weighed on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Other than corporate activity, we saw little trade from other market participants today," said a city bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  LOSE OR GAIN? \par \par \uc2  Most economists anticipate an ECB rate cut despite worries about the strong money supply data, arguing that the ECB itself had played down the rise in M3 money supply by saying they were inflated by special factors. \par \par \uc2  But some market players, unsettled by the rise in M3, took remarks from Bundesbank President and ECB council member Ernst Welteke as a sign that the ECB may refrain from taking action this time. \par \par \uc2  Welteke said on Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's focus was on price stability, not on stimulating growth. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were likewise divided on how the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may react after the ECB's decision. \par \par \uc2  "If the ECB does cut rates, that will be a strong support for the euro and it could rise convincingly above $0.9150 in a very short time period," Muramatsu said. \par \par \uc2  But others were sceptical and said a lack of convincing bids after a rate cut could spur active long liquidation. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that the euro is standing below $0.91 now indicates the market's doubts about seeing a sharp rally," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still long of euros after having factored in a credit easing by the ECB," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers agreed the euro will come under strong selling pressure should the ECB refrain from acting at Thursday's meeting, although they were unsure about the degree of such selling. \par \par \uc2  Muramatsu said selling might not be all that dramatic since the market already had a low opinion of the ECB and the U.S. economic outlook was looking gloomier and gloomier. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR STILL WEIGHED \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to regain some support after the closely watched revised second-quarter U.S. gross domestic product (GDP) came in above expectations of zero growth. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. economy grew at its slowest pace in eight years, with GDP growth revised down to a 0.2 percent annual rate from a preliminary reading of 0.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar struggled to rise against the yen , showing muted reaction to a slew of factors bearish for the yen on Thursday, such as the weaker-than-expected Japanese output figures and continued falls in Tokyo share prices. \par \par \uc2  Japan's industrial output fell for a fifth straight month in July, slipping 2.8 percent from a month earlier, worse than a median forecast of a 2.4 percent decline in a Reuters survey of 13 economists conducted last week. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda also tried to talk down the yen, but the market showed little reaction. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said he saw the current yen level as strange and not good for the Japanese economy, adding that it was not reflecting economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  Warnings also emerged from a powerful Japanese business lobby. \par \par \uc2  Takashi Imai, chairman of the Japan Federation of Economic Organisations (Keidanren) said on Thursday a further rise in the yen would hurt an already fragile economy, calling on the government to coordinate with other countries to curb its strength. \par \par \uc2  Imai said he thought that recent comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill suggested the United States may have changed its strong-dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Wariness about possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also a factor, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  In offshore trade on Wednesday, the dollar dropped as low as 119.08 yen after breaking below key technical support of 119.70 yen, but rumours of a BOJ rate check induced active short-covering around the lows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0545 GMT, the dollar stood at 119.92/93 yen against Wednesday's U.S. close of 120.11/21 yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010830dx8u00cyt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
