\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro hit as focus shifts to ECB rate cut prospects. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dhara Ranasinghe \par \uc2 711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2001\par 14:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 28 (Reuters) - The euro shed almost one percent against the dollar on Tuesday after data showing stronger than expected euro zone money supply growth stoked worries the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may delay any growth-boosting rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  Its biggest one-day fall in almost two months against the dollar also pushed the euro to two-week troughs against the yen. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  halved earlier losses against the dollar after the Bank of Japan said markets decide \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and it was not possible for the authorities to guide the yen lower. \par \par \uc2  The ECB played down the M3 money supply jump, which it said was fuelled by special factors. But with markets still long of euros after chasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to five-month peaks against the dollar last week, the euro was expected to stay under pressure ahead of Thursday's ECB rate decision. \par \par \uc2  "Markets are nervous ahead of the ECB meeting and the M3 data has added to this nervousness," said Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London. \par \par \uc2  "But we have seen six weeks of gains in the euro and it is not surprising that the market is now pausing for breath." \par \par \uc2  The euro hit two-week lows around $0.9030, recording its biggest one-day loss since July 6, when the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to eight-month troughs against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  It was pinned at two-week lows around 108.65 yen at 1158 GMT, down about half a percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers looked forward to U.S. consumer confidence data due at 1400 GMT for further clues to the state of the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  ECB RATE DEBATE \par \par \uc2  July M3 money supply rose an annual seasonally adjusted 6.4 percent, compared with expectations of an unchanged figure at 6.1 percent. But the ECB played down the jump, saying it was inflated by investors seeking refuge from volatile stock markets by moving into low-risk, short term paper. \par \par \uc2  Still, the M3 data is viewed as a barometer of inflationary pressures and the strong numbers may have disappointed some, following recent signs that inflation in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone may be abating. \par \par \uc2  In a Reuters poll taken last week, 41 out of 55 economists forecast the ECB would cut the minimum bid refi rate by a quarter point to 4.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's reluctance to adopt a growth-boosting interest rate policy was cited by fund managers as a major reason for shunning euro zone assets earlier in the year. \par \par \uc2  "The euro is likely to drift lower into Thursday," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns International in London. \par \par \uc2  "At the same time, I feel the M3 data is a red herring and the ECB will cut rates. Any disappointment seen today should be unwound after Thursday." \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was still long of euros and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come under further selling pressure. Data from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange shows net long euro positioning at a record high in the week to Tuesday August 21. \par \par \uc2  The euro was steady following news earlier the ECB's weekly liquidity tender dipped to the 4.50 percent floor as money market players positioned themselves for an ECB rate cut this week. \par \par \uc2  MIXED YEN TALK \par \par \uc2  The yen trimmed its losses against the dollar after Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami's supportive comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Hayami's remarks contrasted with earlier comments by Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa that he expected calls for a weaker yen within Japan to emerge ahead of the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting in late September. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 120.10 yen, down over a third of a percent from the day's peaks but still up slightly from Monday's New York close. \par \par \uc2  Markets have been wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap export-crimping yen strength after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally to 2-1/2 month peaks against the dollar earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Earlier news that Japan's unemployment rate rose to a record 5.0 percent in July, had little immediate impact on the yen as it had been widely expected. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010828dx8s00p62\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
