\par\par \b \uc2  Japanese Jawboning On Yen Falls Mostly On Deaf Ears \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2001\par 21:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Japan's top finance officials brought a new level of transparency to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight Tuesday in an effort to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  But by the end of trading in New York it seemed clear that their jawboning had fallen mostly on deaf ears. \par \par \uc2  U.S.-based analysts say that despite the fact that Japan is flirting with its fourth recession in a decade, the yen is still much more likely to strengthen further against the dollar, buoyed by repatriation of yen by Japanese banks, dollar sales by Japanese exporters and investors selling short-yen positions in a climate of general dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  This is occurring even as most economists would agree that the yen is extremely overvalued against the dollar on a fundamental basis and that it should trade at levels closer to Y130. \par \par \uc2  "Every day Kuroda and Shiokawa are out there saying the yen should reflect fundamentals and then every day the yen strengthens as exporters keep quashing the dollar," said Bob Keiser, analyst at MCM \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Watch in New York. \par \par \uc2  In surprisingly stark language, both finance minister Masajuro Shiokawa and vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda said in Tokyo that they want a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , threatening to "take measures" to reach that goal, if necessary. \par \par \uc2  "Putting aside the question of whether Japan will seek agreement for a weaker yen from other Group of Seven nations, we are hoping the yen will get weaker," Shiokawa said at a regular press conference. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda, Japan's top financial diplomat, who is usually more guarded than Shiokawa, was equally blunt. \par \par \uc2  "We will watch the situation more, but we may have to take measures," if the yen appreciates further, he said, adding that he doesn't think the current level of the yen against the U.S. dollar is appropriate. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Bluntness Shock Some Market Watchers\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The outright bluntness of the comments, in terms of the government's\par desire for a weaker yen and its willingness to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , certainly\par shocked U.S.-based market watchers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I was surprised there wasn't much of a (market) reaction," said\par Robert Sinche, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citibank in New York. "I\par thought Kuroda's comments were almost more important and revealing (than\par Shiokawa's) in terms of what they're looking at."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar did jump about half a yen earlier in the global session to\par a high of Y120.23 from Y119.74 on the remarks and managed to extend\par those gains a bit in New York trading. But after hitting an intraday\par high of Y120.79, the dollar slipped quickly back again to around\par Y120.11.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yen has gained almost 5% against the dollar since early July,\par largely shrugging off a monetary easing by the Bank of Japan in the\par process.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Clearly, government officials are frustrated that the yen remains\par stubbornly firm against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With the country again teetering on the brink of recession, a weaker\par yen would help inject a jolt of life into the economy by making Japanese\par exports more competitive internationally.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It would also offer one of the few means available to stimulate\par growth, as there is little more the Bank of Japan can do to ease credit\par via interest rates, which are now effectively at zero.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The stronger yen has rallied support in Japan for a major BOJ\par 'quantitative easing' program, under which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would flood\par the banking system with money by buying Japanese government bonds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The aim would be to pull Japan out of its deflationary cycle, by using\par money supply to stabilize the core consumer price index at zero or\par slightly above in year-on-year terms. Advocates of this approach say the\par BOJ should adopt an explicit inflation target and steer monetary policy\par to that level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For some analysts, jawboning - or even outright \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - won't\par have a whole lot of influence until \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  implements such a policy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Relative Thrust Of Monetary Policy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's clear to us that the authorities want the yen to weaken, but the\par problem is the yen is really driven by the relative thrust of monetary\par policy," said Mike Rosenberg, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at\par Deutsche Bank in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Even though the BOJ is easing, the U.S. is easing more aggressively\par at the moment."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rosenberg said the BOJ will have to "over-ease relative to what the\par Federal Reserve is doing" if they want a weaker yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On a shorter-term perspective, the yen is also getting support from\par capital flows into Japan, which, according to Citibank, `have turned\par decisively in favor of the yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Japanese exporters, traditional sellers of dollar/yen, have actively\par accelerated their selling and Japanese real money investors are\par sporadically bringing funds back to Japan," Citibank said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank says this could propel the yen to around 118.25 against the\par dollar in the near term, a level that many analysts think would spark\par actual, rather than verbal, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Japanese authorities.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381;\par grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010828dx8s0015r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
