\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Shiokawa comments give yen a boost pre-FOMC. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2001\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 21 (Reuters) - The yen rose a quarter of a percent against the dollar and half a percent against the euro on Tuesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to rule out any immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. \par \par \uc2  But traders said market conditions were very illiquid ahead of the Federal Reserve interest rate decision at 1815 GMT. The Fed is widely expected to trim its benchmark lending rate by 25 basis points but dealers were alert to the possibility of a bigger cut. \par \par \uc2  The yen got a lift after Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said he was not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets now, easing the concerns of dealers who have become nervous in recent days that Japan would try to bring the yen down to help its exporters. \par \par \uc2  "The comments from Shiokawa suggesting they are in no rush to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave the yen a bit of support," said Francesca Fornasari, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "But things are very quiet, everyone is simply waiting for the FOMC." \par \par \uc2  At 0733 GMT, the dollar was 120.40 yen , down a third of a yen on the day while the euro was hovering just above the psychologically significant 110 yen level . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was steady against the dollar around $0.9140, barely changed from the New York close, after testing the donwside near $0.91 earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  WAITING FOR THE FED \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that most people expected the Fed to cut the target for its funds rate by a a quarter point to 3.5 percent but a larger cut could not be ruled out altogether given that there were still no convincing signs of a rebound. \par \par \uc2  But they said the dollar's reaction to a bigger cut was difficult to call as while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might rally as U.S. equities responded favourably to lower borrowing costs, the combination of lower yield and fears the Fed knew of more bad news to come might push the greenback down. \par \par \uc2  "The equity market might rally on a 50bps rate cut and initially push the dollar up but the move could also be seen as panicky, it might hint at desperation," said Adrian Schmidt, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE JAWBONING \par \par \uc2  Despite his initial comments suggesting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Shiokawa later said the MOF might act if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were too "rapid" and even raised the spectre of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the market is far from convinced it could get the avowedly non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  administration of U.S. President George W. Bush to act, never mind the reclusive European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Later still, MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda weighed in, warning that the ministry was watching the market closely and stood ready to act as needed. \par \par \uc2  Traders warned the market had turned so bullish on the euro so quickly the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a victim of its own success. \par \par \uc2  A quarterly survey of 94 global fund managers conducted by Merrill Lynch found the third-lowest exposure to dollars and their third-highest weighting of euros - or the previous equivalent - in the entire 12-year history of the series. \par \par \uc2  This follows data on futures contracts from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange last week showing a substantial build-up in net long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  While that could be taken as a sign of renewed confidence in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was also a potential stumbling block. \par \par \uc2  With so many investors already long, they may not be keen to buy yet more euros while the lure of profits would always be there to tempt them to sell. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted previous fund manager surveys had shown the market was structurally long of the euro earlier in the year and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  duly performed badly. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dx8l03tct\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
