\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar tired by MOF verbal gymnastics, awaits Fed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2001\par 04:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 21 (Reuters) - The dollar benefited from some last minute short-covering on Tuesday as investors took cover ahead of a Federal Reserve policy meeting which is universally expected to sanction the seventh cut in interest rates this year. \par \par \uc2  Trade was down to a trickle but Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa managed to cause a stir by saying he was not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets now. \par \par \uc2  While this jibed with dealers' suspicions that the MOF would not act unless the yen threatened to break the 118.00/30 area, they were still surprised he would be so candid since the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone had been enough to arrest the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  "Keeping markets uncertain is half the battle, so telling them you aren't going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a tactical blunder," said a mystified U.S. bank dealer. "He's lucky everybody's on hold for the Fed meeting or the reaction would have been much larger." \par \par \uc2  Perhaps with this in mind, Shiokawa later added that the MOF did not intend to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves became rapid, leaving the market guessing as to what "rapid" entailed. \par \par \uc2  He also raised the spectre of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was always in touch with other nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But the market is far from convinced it could get the avowedly non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bush administration to act, no matter the reclusive European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Later still, MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda weighed in warning that the Ministry was watching the market closely and stood ready to act as needed. \par \par \uc2  The net result of all the verbal gymnastics was to leave the dollar dithering in a tiny 120.72/120.92 yen range though that is at least well above last week's two-month lows at 119.05. \par \par \uc2  The euro was equally aimless at 110.20 yen having backed away from 3-= month highs at 110.63 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also faded to $0.9124 after failing to sustain a rally above 92 cents on Monday. Traders reported good bids at $0.9110 with more at $0.9090/95 chart support, including Asian banks defending stop-loss positions. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts cautioned that a break under $0.9090/95 would confirm a double top on the daily charts and could trigger a correction toward $0.9030. \par \par \uc2  AWAITING SENTENCE \par \par \uc2  Much depends on the Fed's meeting today with a cut of 25 basis points so well discounted that anything else would come as a major shock. \par \par \uc2  Analysts assume the risk, if anything, is for a half-point cut but most doubt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would want to return to such an aggressive policy after only shifting to smaller moves at the last meeting. \par \par \uc2  They also assume the bank will keep its implicit easing bias by saying that the risks are weighted toward economic weakness. Dropping this bias would imply no more cuts and could cause considerable harm to Wall Street and consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  Which leaves only a paragraph or so in the Fed's statement to cover the economic outlook, and anything that dwelt on the impediments to recovery may prove a sore spot for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Thus many analysts expect a repeat of the terse June statement, where the Fed used just two sentences to describe the main areas of economic weakness and why inflation was likely to be contained. \par \par \uc2  IN A CHINA SHOP \par \par \uc2  Traders warned the market had turned so bullish on the euro so quickly the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a victim of its own success. \par \par \uc2  A quarterly survey of 94 global fund managers conducted by Merrill Lynch found the third-lowest exposure to dollars and their third-highest weighting of euros - or the previous equivalent - in the entire 12-year history of the series. \par \par \uc2  This follows data on futures contracts from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange last week showing a substantial build-up in net long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  While that could be taken as a sign of renewed confidence in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was also a potential stumbling block. \par \par \uc2  With so many investors already long, they may not be keen to buy yet more euros while the lure of profits would always be there to tempt them to sell. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted previous fund manager surveys had shown the market was structurally long of the euro earlier in the year and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  duly performed badly. \par \par \uc2  It was only when they finally gave up and cleared out - around mid-year - that the euro began to rise. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dx8l03cq3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
