\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Major  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  take cover for Fed decision. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Isabel Reynolds \par \uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2001\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 21 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped slightly in subdued trade on Tuesday as nervous dealers awaited a Federal Reserve policy meeting expected to agree the seventh cut in U.S. interest rates this year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said consensus for a 25 percentage point rate cut was so well factored in that if Tuesday's meeting proved their forecast correct, the dollar could well fall in its aftermath. \par \par \uc2  But there was still a faint possibility that the Fed could surprise the market, some analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "I guess there's a 20 percent or so risk that they'll go 50 basis points, instead of 25. There is some potential for them to do a bigger cut, so that's one reason for caution," said Ron Leven, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  He added that although most market players knew what they expected of the Fed, they were having a harder time working out what reaction to expect from the markets. \par \par \uc2  Trade was down to a trickle but Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa managed to cause a stir by saying he was not considering \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets now. \par \par \uc2  He later added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) did not intend to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were rapid, leaving the market guessing as to the definition of "rapid". \par \par \uc2  He also raised the spectre of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was always in touch with other nations on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE JAWBONING \par \par \uc2  But the market is far from convinced it could get the avowedly non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  administration of U.S. President George W. Bush to act, never mind the reclusive European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Later still, MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda weighed in, warning that the ministry was watching the market closely and stood ready to act as needed. \par \par \uc2  The net result of all the verbal gymnastics was to leave the dollar dithering in a tiny 120.62/120.92 yen range , although that is well above last week's two-month lows at 119.05. \par \par \uc2  The euro was equally aimless at 110.12 yen , having backed away from 16-week highs at 110.63 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also faded to $0.9128 after failing to sustain a rally above 92 cents on Monday. Traders reported good bids at $0.9110 with more at $0.9090/95 chart support, including Asian banks defending stop-loss positions. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts cautioned that a break under $0.9090/95 would confirm a double top on the daily charts and could trigger a correction toward $0.9030. \par \par \uc2  Much depends on the Fed's meeting today with a cut of 25 basis points so well discounted that anything else would come as a major shock. \par \par \uc2  Analysts assume the bank will keep its implicit easing bias by saying the risks are weighted toward economic weakness. Dropping this bias would imply no more cuts and could cause considerable harm to Wall Street and consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  IN A CHINA SHOP \par \par \uc2  Traders warned the market had turned so bullish on the euro so quickly the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become a victim of its own success. \par \par \uc2  A quarterly survey of 94 global fund managers conducted by Merrill Lynch found the third-lowest exposure to dollars and their third-highest weighting of euros - or the previous equivalent - in the entire 12-year history of the series. \par \par \uc2  This follows data on futures contracts from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange last week showing a substantial build-up in net long euro positions. \par \par \uc2  While that could be taken as a sign of renewed confidence in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it was also a potential stumbling block. \par \par \uc2  With so many investors already long, they may not be keen to buy yet more euros while the lure of profits would always be there to tempt them to sell. \par \par \uc2  Analysts noted previous fund manager surveys had shown the market was structurally long of the euro earlier in the year and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  duly performed badly. \par \par \uc2  It was only when they finally gave up and cleared out - around mid-year - that the euro began to rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0541GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                     Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                              20 Aug.\par                     -----------------------------------------\par   Euro/dlr              0.9128   0.9145   -0.19    -3.88   0.9496\par   Dlr/yen               120.63   120.71   -0.07    +5.40   114.45\par   Euro/yen              110.14   110.52   -0.34    +1.32   108.71\par   Dlr/swiss             1.6621   1.6606   +0.09    +3.80   1.6012\par   Stg/dlr               1.4444   1.4441   +0.02    -3.71   1.5000\par   Can/dlr               1.5447   1.5447   +0.00    +3.57   1.4914\par   Aus/dlr               0.5344   0.5326 