\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Shiokawa says not mulling  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  now. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2001\par 07:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 21 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa on Tuesday warned against the yen's recent rise but dashed market speculation that Japan may take imminent action to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-crippling strength. \par \par \uc2  "The market is the one to decide (\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels). But in the past few days, the yen's moves have been rapid and I don't think they reflected the fundamentals of Japan and other nations," he told reporters after appearing on a television talk show. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time Shiokawa, who has been on holiday, had commented on recent movements in the yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit two-month highs of around 119.20 per dollar last week. \par \par \uc2  The sharp rise in the yen has helped to fuel a downward spiral on the stock market, hitting shares of car makers and electronics manufacturers. A stronger yen dents exporters' overseas earnings when they are repatriated. \par \par \uc2  Regarding speculation in the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, Shiokawa said: "I am not thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but I am watching it with great interest. \par \par \uc2  "If the situation becomes abnormal, I think we need to consult with other nations. I think (Vice Finance Minister Haruhiko) Kuroda has always been consulting with other nations and keeping in touch with them." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa put it more directly a little later, telling reporters: "We may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if conditions become abnormal." \par \par \uc2  The yen was briefly unsettled after Shiokawa's initial remarks, reversing some of its early weakness against the dollar. Traders said they were surprised the finance minister had been so candid, since the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone had been keeping the yen's rise in check. \par \par \uc2  While dealers interpreted his remarks as leaving the door open to concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said it was unlikely given recent differences in view between Japan, the United States and the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was fetching 120.65 yen at 0543 GMT, but trade was thin with many players at the sidelines before the Federal Reserve's policy-setting meeting later in the day. \par \par \uc2  CREATING INFLATION 'DANGEROUS' \par \par \uc2  Economists said the impact of the recent rise in the yen had been limited so far, but if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened sharply, it could start hurting the world's number-two economy. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is in a downturn and the yen's weakness has been supporting it through exporters," said Akimasa Okada, an economist at NLI Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  "But if the yen rises further, it could worsen the deflationary situation in the economy." \par \par \uc2  Okada said many Japanese firms would be comfortable with a dollar rate above 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key "tankan" corporate survey released in July, big Japanese manufacturers have planned their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a dollar rate of 114.05 yen in mind for the current fiscal year to next March. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he welcomed the Bank of Japan's (BOJ) decision to conduct further easing last week, adding that it was up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to decide monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The government and politicians have been pressuring the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease further to help the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  Some politicians have been urging it to adopt an inflation target to boost the flagging economy and stem deflation - an idea the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has so far resisted. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said that setting a target to create inflation would be dangerous and harmful to the Japanese economy, although he said he did want to see domestic prices rise. \par \par \uc2  "Although it could work temporarily, maintaining such a policy would cause runaway inflation and send the economy spinning out of control," he told the television talk show. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ last Tuesday eased credit further by raising the target for bank reserves to six trillion yen ($49 billion) from five trillion, partly by boosting its monthly purchases of Japanese government bonds to 600 billion yen from 400 billion. (Additional reporting by Yonggi Kang, Ritsuko Ando). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dx8l01b34\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
