\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Debate over radical BOJ policy change heats up. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2001\par 10:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - Debate over how the Bank of Japan could stop the nation's price deflation and an economic slowdown heated up on Friday, with politicians and even some central bankers suggesting unorthodox, possibly risky, policy ideas. \par \par \uc2  A group of lawmakers from the ruling Liberal Democratic Party renewed their call for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to create a certain level of inflation, while the minutes of last month's BOJ policy board meeting showed one member favoured a weaker yen to stem deflation plaguing the world's second-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Only a day earlier, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami ruled out such choices, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being pushed to the ropes after easing its policy on Tuesday even though its previous easing in March had pushed down interest rates to virtually zero. \par \par \uc2  "What is important is for the BOJ to shift its policy so that it eradicates expectations of deflation," said Yoichi Masuzoe, a newly elected Upper House member of the LDP who has been leading the campaign for an inflation-targeting policy. \par \par \uc2  The LDP group criticised the BOJ for limiting itself to piecemeal easing and dared Hayami to face its members at a public debate on inflation targeting. \par \par \uc2  Masuzoe said last week the BOJ should aim for an inflation rate of around three, four percent. Japan's consumer price index fell by 0.6 percent from a year earlier in June for the 21st consecutive month of decline. \par \par \uc2  Hayami has repeatedly played down the feasibility of a policy to fanning inflation, and the minutes of the BOJ's July 12-13 Policy Board meeting released on Friday showed that most members agreed it was inappropriate. \par \par \uc2  However, one member did say the BOJ should consider it, while another member said the BOJ's current policy framework had a similar effect anyway. \par \par \uc2  Under the current framework, known as quantitative easing, the BOJ floods the money market with excess liquidity so that funds pile up in banks' current account deposits at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which act as a base for their credit generation. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ on Tuesday raised the target for the current accounts to six trillion yen ($49.8 billion) from five trillion, partly by boosting its monthly purchases of Japanese government bonds (JGBs) to 600 billion yen from 400 billion. \par \par \uc2  LDP's Masuzoe said the BOJ should raise the target to 10 trillion yen and buy 800 billion of JGBs. Last week, he said the BOJ should even buy stocks or real estate if need be. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  IMPACT \par \par \uc2  Pressure was also mounting on the BOJ to consider the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates on prices and growth, particularly after the yen's big rise this week. \par \par \uc2  Some board members at the July meeting had said it would be appropriate to leave the yen to fall of its own accord, and one member even said it would be difficult to halt deflation without a weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  A weaker yen would result in higher import prices, helping to ease deflation while also helping Japanese exporters. \par \par \uc2  Some economists suggest the BOJ should buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen because in doing so it would provide yen funds to the private sector. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said on Thursday that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was in the government's hands, not the BOJ's, and that driving down the yen could even backfire as neighbouring Asian economies would be destabilised, thereby hurting Japan's own export markets. \par \par \uc2  Instead of weakening, however, the yen shot up more than three percent against the dollar in just two days this week, hitting a 2-= month high before retreating somewhat. \par \par \uc2  Yoshimi Watanabe, a member of the LDP group, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen strengthened despite a weak Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010817dx8h00gtr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
