\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar gets respite as MOF baits the bears. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2001\par 04:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - The dollar was enjoying a much-needed respite on Friday with would-be sellers discouraged for the moment by the threat of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi on Friday said movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been speculative and excessive. \par \par \uc2  News that MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda had cut short his holidays to return to Tokyo also heightened tension. Kuroda has already warned that the Ministry stood ready to take appropriate action, a standard phrase that has often preceded \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. \par \par \uc2  However, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaru Hayami was less emphatic when he discussed the yen on Thursday, saying a higher yen was not good for exporters but also that a much weaker yen would trouble Japan's Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  Hayami also brushed aside calls for the BOJ to buy foreign assets in order to pump money into the domestic economy while weakening the yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also hearing talk that actual \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely until and unless the dollar fell to 115.00 yen, though others thought the MOF might try and defend 118.30, the low from the dollar's last pullback in June. \par \par \uc2  Still, the threat alone was enough to keep the dollar supported at 120.30 yen some way above the week's 119.05 trough and in line with New York's late 120.36. \par \par \uc2  Traders said dollar/yen was also sandwiched between M&A-related bids at 119.50 and offers around 120.50. The former was rumoured to be related to Furukawa Electric Co's purchase of Lucent Technologies' fibre-optics business, and the latter to Fuji Bank's sale of its stake in Heller Financial in the United States. \par \par \uc2  Yen sentiment was weighted at the margin by a dip in the Nikkei share average to fresh 16-year lows. \par \par \uc2  However, dealers say the effect is two-edged since the market always speculates that financial distress at home will prompt some Japanese institutions to repatriate funds from offshore. \par \par \uc2  BEAR TRAP \par \par \uc2  The euro was taking a time-out at $0.9130 having rallied five percent in little more than a week. \par \par \uc2  It did take a peek above 92 cents in New York but traders found that rumoured stop-loss buy orders where in fact just rumours and they quickly took profits. \par \par \uc2  Favourable U.S. inflation and housing data accelerated the pullback and saw it as low as $0.9090 support at one point. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar was rocked anew by a dismal economic report from the Philadelphia Federal Reserve. The Philly Fed's main business conditions index dived to minus 25.3 in August from minus 12.2 the month before, way below analysts' forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The market is now awaiting U.S. consumer confidence and trade data later on Friday to offer fresh direction for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Then again dealers saw a potential bear trap opening for the euro in the shape of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  The market has been entertaining increasing hopes the ECB would not only ease at its next meeting on August 30, but cut by a full 50 basis points. This would be regarded as a positive for economic growth and thus the euro. \par \par \uc2  However, a monthly report on the German economy from the Bundesbank released on Thursday argued that the ECB had been right not to cut rates even though German growth had slowed to a standstill in the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Buba went on to say that monetary and the broader economic situation spoke in favour of a steady-as-she-goes interest-rate policy. \par \par \uc2  "The market's pinned a lot on the idea of an ECB easing but the risk is it will be disappointed, particularly on the idea of a half-point cut," said a European Bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "If so, that could be a big stumbling block for the euro, especially as the market's pricing in at least two more cuts from the Fed this year," he added. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010817dx8h00fa5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
