\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar veers away from 5-month lows against euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Atiya Hussain \par \uc2 817 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 19:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates with U.S. prices, data. Changes dateline, byline. Previously London) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Aug 16 (Reuters) - After a dizzying downward spiral that has taken it to five-month lows against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar found its feet on Thursday after better than expected U.S. housing, inflation and unemployment data. \par \par \uc2  "Inflation came in about as expected ... but housing clearly is strong, much more than expected. Lower interest rates and the persistence of historically low unemployment are lending support to the broader economy," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York. \par \par \uc2  "In the short-run, it should help the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  The consumer price index (CPI) fell 0.3 percent in July, against expectations for unchanged, as energy prices fell even more than had been expected. \par \par \uc2  First-time jobless claims fell to 380,000 in the week ending Aug. 11, pulling the less volatile four-week average down to its lowest level since early March, raising hopes the U.S. labor market may have seen the worst of the economic downturn. Housing starts in July far ex??? rising 2.8 percent to a 1.672 million annual rate, compared to the forecast 1.627 million level. \par \par \uc2  But the supportive data did not turn the tide of the market's dollar-bearishness, which has clipped the dollar's strength as measured by the broad-based trade-weighed Finex index by nearly seven percent in the past month. Much of the fall followed investor realization that the widely touted U.S. economic recovery is still far from imminent. \par \par \uc2  "The sentiment is still pretty uniform along the side of dollar-bearishness," said Michael Donahue, senior vice president at Fimat USA in New York. \par \par \uc2  EURO CORRECTS AFTER STEEP RALLY \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro, which has run up more than 4-1/2 cents against the dollar in the past week alone, was overbought and ripe for profit-taking on Thursday. The euro's rally is even more impressive given that little over a month ago the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at its lows for the year near 83.35 cents - little more than a cent above its all-time lows. \par \par \uc2  In early U.S. trading, the euro slipped nearly half a cent, to dip below 91 cents before steadying around 91.10 cents, down 0.30 percent from the previous U.S. close and well below the five-month peaks above 92 cents hit overnight. \par \par \uc2  "I think we're going to be in a correction mode for the next few days, and that's a healthy thing considering the move we've had over the last 30 days. I don't think there's anything odd about that, but we need to get a feel for how much of a correction it's going to be," said John Hazelton, trader at PNC Bank. \par \par \uc2  Sterling also retreated from its five-month peaks above $1.45 against the bedraggled dollar, to trade around $1.4425, still up a hair from the previous U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also veered off the five-month lows near 1.6530 francs hit against the Swiss franc overnight, to trade around 1.6670 francs, up 0.35 percent from the previous U.S. close. \par \par \uc2  KURODA'S VEILED \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  THREATS \par \par \uc2  The dollar also pushed back above 120 yen in early U.S. trading, regaining more than a full yen from the 2-1/2 month lows the greenback fell to on Wednesday. The dollar's tumble surprised \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and analysts, coming a day after the Bank of Japan's unexpected decision to loosen already ultra-easy monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, the dollar was boosted above the 120 yen level after comments by Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda, who said the yen's rapid rise this week was inappropriate, especially immediately after the Bank of Japan eased monetary policy, and said Japan stood ready to take appropriate steps. A stronger yen could hamper Japanese exports at a time when the country's economy needs all the help it can get to pull out of its dire economic state. \par \par \uc2  The veiled threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only gave the dollar a brief fillip in Asian trading, as traders said the dollar would need to fall further against the yen before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a fear in Asian trade but this is not likely at these levels and the dollar could go down further," said Bobby Jasper, senior trader at Fuji Bank. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, meanwhile, took a noncommittal stance on the yen's rapid rise, saying he did not think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets as part of its easing policy. Some economists and politicians have suggested the BOJ should buy such assets, a move that would likely weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00urk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
