\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds from 5-month low versus euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 14:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The dollar recouped some ground on Thursday after early losses took its fall this week against the euro to five percent and it reached five-month lows against other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders attributed its gains to profit taking and market wariness ahead of U.S. economic data due later in the day. \par \par \uc2  "New York traders are probably seeing these levels as a good time to sell the euro," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  Concern over the state of the U.S. economy and the Bush administration's commitment to a strong dollar policy weighed on the dollar earlier in the session. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer price data for July and the Philadelphia Fed sentiment survey for August are due later, and are expected to provide new direction for the markets. \par \par \uc2  LISTENING TO O'NEILL \par \par \uc2  Analysts and dealers said Washington's strong dollar policy remained a focus after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill failed to give a resounding confirmation of it on Wednesday. In a CNBC interview, O'Neill said the U.S. has "a continuing, continuous policy". \par \par \uc2  The White House and the Treasury also moved on Wednesday to publicly deny speculation that the U.S. government may soften its commitment to a strong dollar, which it inherited from the administration of Bill Clinton. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that against a backdrop of weaker U.S. data, a break of key technical levels had encouraged speculative investors to buy European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the dollar in thin August markets. \par \par \uc2  "We had a big sell-off in euro/dollar in May and June, and with a natural bottoming-out of prices, speculative funds have been caught short," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. "The move starts to establish its own momentum." \par \par \uc2  But the mood could change again once investors returned from their holidays next month, analysts added, particularly if German data proved disappointing. \par \par \uc2  German growth came to a halt in the second quarter of this year, with gross domestic product at about the same level as in the first quarter, the Bundesbank said in its August monthly report. It also said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "stable hand" monetary policy had been appropriate. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said on Thursday Germany's economic conditions were "unsatisfactory", but he was nevertheless optimistic growth would start to pick up again next year. \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakened beyond $0.92 per euro for the first time since March, according to Reuters data, before recovering to around $0.9155 by 1145 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also fell to five-month lows against the Swiss franc around 1.6530 before recouping ground to 1.66, and to five-month lows against sterling beyond $1.45, before retracing. \par \par \uc2  CAUTION ON YEN \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady within half a yen of Wednesday's 2-1/2 month lows near 119 yen after getting a brief fillip in Asia following comments by Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said the yen's rapid rise this week was inappropriate and Japan stood ready to take appropriate steps. \par \par \uc2  But traders and analysts said the dollar would need to fall further against the yen before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a fear in Asian trade but this is not likely at these levels and the dollar could go down further," said Bobby Jasper, senior trader at Fuji Bank. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, meanwhile, said he did not think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets as part of its easing policy. Some economists and politicians have suggested the BOJ should buy such assets, a move that would likely weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  U.S. ECONOMY WOES \par \par \uc2  Data on Wednesday did little to alleviate the gloomy picture of the U.S. economy painted a day earlier by the International Monetary Fund. July industrial production fell for the 10th straight month, while U.S. business sales fell 1.4 percent in June, the steepest fall since August 1992. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer price data for July are due at 1230 GMT, and are forecast to be unchanged from the previous month. Prices excluding food and energy are forecast to have risen by 0.2 percent. July housing starts and weekly jobless claims are also due at 1230 GMT, and the Philadelphia Fed survey for August at 1600 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00m2l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
