\par\par \b \uc2  POLL- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen before end Sep unlikely. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pratima Desai \par \uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 13:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The fragile Japanese economy could do without the recent appreciation of the yen against the dollar, but the government is unlikely to order actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the near term, a Reuters survey of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists showed. \par \par \uc2  The median of 24 responses in the survey, taken Wednesday and Thursday, put the probability the Bank of Japan would step in to weaken the yen before the end of September at 30 percent. Instead the government and the BoJ would first attempt to talk the yen down, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Only if this fails will the government contemplate actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  resorts to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the likely dollar/yen level is 117, the median of 23 forecasts showed. Forecasts ranged between 110.00 and 120.00. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step up...at current levels with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is likely to get more hawkish as dollar/yen is pushed below 120," said Peter Luxton at S&P MMS. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has traded in a range between 119.29 yen and 120.25 yen so far on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Typically the MoF and BoJ go through the motions before jumping in to sell the yen and we look for the same pattern to be repeated this time," said Luxton. \par \par \uc2  BoJ Governor Masaru Hayami started the ball rolling earlier on Thursday. He said it would be desirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to reflect economic fundamentals and be stable and that too strong a yen would be negative for Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  GREEN LIGHT \par \par \uc2  At the end of July Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa gave the green light to a natural decline in the yen. \par \par \uc2  With an effective zero interest rate policy and public debt the worst among industrial nations, analysts say a weaker yen is one of the last policy options available to help boost the economy by making exports more competitive. \par \par \uc2  Forecasts for the likelihood of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ranged between five and 95 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The chances of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increase the stronger the yen gets. What \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs now is not a strong yen," said Jesper Fischer-Nielsen at Danske Bank in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts in the survey said much depends on the so-called "strong dollar" policy of the United States, instigated in 1995 by then U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin after the dollar fell to a record low of 79.70 yen. \par \par \uc2  Confusion about the U.S. administration's intent with regard to the policy and weaker-then-expected economic data from the United States have been behind the dollar fall to 119.05 yen on Wednesday, the lowest since June 5. \par \par \uc2  It has allowed the U.S. Federal Reserve to ease aggressively this year by 2.75 percentage points to 3.75 percent. But now the easing cycle is probably nearing an end and the need for a strong dollar really isn't there, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  COMFORT LEVELS \par \par \uc2  Some of those who gave a greater than 50 percent chance of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the end of September said the government wants to see dollar/yen in a range between 120 and 125 where it has mostly traded in the last two months. \par \par \uc2  "To be fair it has traded more often nearer the top of the range...120 will make them feel really uncomfortable," said Rob Carnell at Commonwealth Bank Australia in London. \par \par \uc2  "The government is asking the BoJ to ease policy specifically to try and weaken the yen and we had that gesture the other day which didn't make a lot of difference." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday expanded its so-called quantitative easing policy by promising more funds to boost bank reserves and by increasing the amount of Japanese government bonds it buys from the market to 600 billion yen from 400 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  "Further easing is useless. Japan is in a liquidity trap. More government spending...will only make consumers more nervous. They will save more and consume less," said Falco van Tartwijk at Fortis Bank in Amsterdam. "Structural reforms are the only way to help Japan get back on its feet." \par \par \uc2  Reform and the promise of them is why some in the survey believe there will be no need for actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because current yen strength will be short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "Money supply is not the problem, the demand for money is. In our view it can only be restored by structural changes in the real economy," said Jukka Koivunen at Okobank in Helsinki. \par \par \uc2  Koivunen said reforms planned by Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi are likely to inflict pain in the short-term on Japan's economy. "This will weaken the yen from current levels." \par \par \uc2  FOR FULL DETAILS OF THE SURVEY DOUBLE-CLICK ON. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00l5j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
