\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits five-month lows versus European  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The dollar fell half a percent to set five-month lows against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday on growing concern over the state of the U.S. economy and the U.S. commitment to a strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was steady within half a percent of Wednesday's 2-1/2 month lows against the yen after getting a brief fillip in Asia following comments by Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs Haruhiko Kuroda. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Washington's strong dollar policy remained a focus after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill failed to give a resounding confirmation of it on Wednesday. In a CNBC interview, O'Neill said the U.S. has "a continuing, continuous policy". \par \par \uc2  "The primary issue for markets is still general dollar weakness and a perceived lack of support from O'Neill's comments," said Bobby Jasper, senior trader at Fuji Bank in London. "There wasn't a blanket endorsement of a strong dollar that we have seen in the past." \par \par \uc2  The White House and the Treasury also moved on Wednesday to publicly deny speculation that the U.S. government may soften its strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar weakened beyond $0.92 per euro for the first time since March, according to Reuters data. Aggressive selling by a U.S. bank hit the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in European trade, traders said, driving it also to five-month troughs around 1.6530 per Swiss franc and five-month lows against sterling beyond $1.45 . \par \par \uc2  The euro has risen every day to make gains of nearly five percent against the dollar in the last week, and has also risen by 10 percent from the year's lows set in early July. On the Finex trade weighted index the dollar has lost almost seven percent since early July, when it was scaling 15-year peaks. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a break of key technical levels had encouraged speculative investors to buy European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the dollar in thin August markets. \par \par \uc2  "We had a big sell-off in euro/dollar in May and June, and with a natural bottoming-out of prices, speculative funds have been caught short," said Ian Gunner, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro. "The move starts to establish its own momentum." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  ALERT \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at the day's lows around 119.30 yen having earlier risen above 120 yen after Japan's chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said the yen's rapid rise this week was inappropriate and Japan stood ready to take appropriate steps. \par \par \uc2  But traders and analysts said the dollar would need to fall further against the yen before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a fear in Asian trade but this is not likely at these levels and the dollar could go down further," said Jasper at Fuji Bank. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, meanwhile, said he did not think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets as part of its easing policy. Some economists and politicians have suggested the BOJ should buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated assets, a move that would likely weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  DATA WATCH \par \par \uc2  Data on Wednesday did little to alleviate the gloomy picture of the U.S. economy painted a day earlier by the International Monetary Fund. July industrial production fell for the 10th straight month, while U.S. business sales fell 1.4 percent in June, the steepest fall since August 1992. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer price data for July are due at 1230 GMT, and are forecast to be unchanged from the previous month. Prices excluding food and energy are forecast to have risen by 0.2 percent. July housing starts and weekly jobless claims are also due at 1230 GMT, and the Philadelphia Fed survey for August at 1600 GMT. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Hans Eichel, meanwhile, said Germany's economic conditions were "unsatisfactory", but he was nevertheless optimistic growth would start to pick up again next year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00iok\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
