\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-BOJ's Hayami non-committal on yen rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 11:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 16 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan (BOJ) Governor Masaru Hayami said on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not buy foreign assets as part of its monetary policy or to sway \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, taking a noncommittal stance against this week's rapid yen rise. \par \par \uc2  Calls on the BOJ to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated assets arose due to worries that it would not be able to pump as much funds into the banking system as it has promised to and that the yen's strengthening would negate the effects of Tuesday's credit easing. \par \par \uc2  Proponents of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  say that by buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from private-sector banks, the BOJ could provide them with yen funds and simultaneously weaken the yen. That would raise import prices and offset deflationary pressure while also helping exporters become more competitive, they say. \par \par \uc2  But Hayami said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not have to go that far to pour ample funds into the yen-denominated banking system, and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to alter \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates was not part of its policy role. \par \par \uc2  "I do not think that we will have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets as part of our (yen) fund provision to meet our monetary policy target," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is only allowed to act as an agent of the government in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hayami noted, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be cautious about what a weaker yen could do to neighbouring Asian economies. \par \par \uc2  "Buying and selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can affect the entire world and it is difficult for one country to make a unilateral decision," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Our neighbouring countries like China, Korea and Taiwan are counting on Japanese markets, and it would be troublesome for them if the yen weakened too much. \par \par \uc2  He also repeated Japanese monetary authorities' standard line on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, saying: "It is desirable for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to reflect economic fundamentals and to be stable." \par \par \uc2  MUM ON FURTHER EASING \par \par \uc2  His remarks came a day after the yen soared to 2-= month highs against the dollar of 119.05 yen despite the BOJ's monetary easing on Tuesday. The Nikkei average of Japanese share prices also struggled near 16-year lows set on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ decided to expand its so-called quantitative easing policy by raising its target for banks' current account deposits to six trillion yen ($50 billion) from five trillion yen. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said it was too early to judge the effect of the policy action on the economy and declined comment on whether the bank could further loosen its credit policy. \par \par \uc2  However, he said that monetary policy alone could not lift the economy out of its current stagnation, placing part of the responsibility on Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi to carry out his pledged structural reforms. \par \par \uc2  Hayami also played down speculation that the BOJ was trying to lower long-term interest rates by promising to buy 600 billion yen in long-term government bonds every month instead of 400 billion yen. \par \par \uc2  While he admitted that an excessive rise in long-term rates would harm the government's credit standings and push up debt-servicing costs, Hayami said the current level of 1.3 to 1.5 percent in the benchmark 10-year bond yield was acceptable. \par \par \uc2  "I think it would be okay if those levels are maintained," he said. (Additional reporting by Ritsuko Ando). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00had\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
