\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -End of an era sighted as dollar turns tail. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wayne Cole \par \uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 08:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 16 (Reuters) - The dollar's pell-mell retreat showed no sign of stopping on Thursday as it hit fresh lows on so many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that dealers were whispering about the end of the strong-dollar era. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cratered at five-month lows on both the euro and the Swiss franc and near a four-month trough on the pound, while the Finex trade weighted index extended its losses to almost seven percent in just the last six weeks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar did manage to claw back some losses on the yen after Japanese Finance Ministry officials put the market on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alert. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Haruhiko Kuroda declared \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood ready to take appropriate steps, a well-worn phrase which has often preceded \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past. \par \par \uc2  Traders were also wary ahead of a news conference by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami since he has roiled markets in the past with comments about the yen's appropriate value. \par \par \uc2  So edgy is the market that a single modest buy order for dollars caused a round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumours and sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  screaming above 120.00 in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The order turned out to be corporate-related rather than official, but the scare was enough to keep the dollar supported at 119.90 yen in late trade. \par \par \uc2  Still that was a world away from the 121.50 level held at the same time on Wednesday and uncomfortably close to its two-month low of 119.05. Traders also reported talk of huge option positions at 119.00, 118.50 and 118.00 yen which would attract speculators. \par \par \uc2  Much of the dollar's decline from 121.00 was linked to stop-loss selling from banks covering exposed option positions, a phenomenon made all the more powerful by a seasonal dearth of liquidity. \par \par \uc2  STERILITY TEST \par \par \uc2  As for the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers noted the dollar was fast approaching the trough of its last pullback in June of 118.26. A dip below that mark would be technically very bearish, opening the way to at least 117.00 if not 114.50, so many dealers suspect the MOF would be keen to defend it. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese government's clearly not happy with the yen strengthening and if the dollar falls much further they will act," said Ron Leven, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "Because the BOJ is already in a quantitative easing mode, they are in a very powerful position since they can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time ever in an unsterilised fashion," he added. \par \par \uc2  In the past the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sterilised its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by soaking up the excess yen it sold in the money market. But the bank is already flooding the banking system with excess reserves of around two trillion yen ($16.68 billion) so there would be no particular reason for it to mop up the yen from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said this would not in itself have much effect on liquidity or interest rates, but it would still resonate in the market as a symbolic commitment to keeping the yen low. \par \par \uc2  EVERY WORD COUNTS \par \par \uc2  Then again, analysts noted that this was not really a case of a strengthening yen but rather of a weakening dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro, for instance, has risen four cents in just six sessions scoring a high of $0.9170 here on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar in turn has shed nine percent on the Swiss franc in the last six weeks to touch 1.6588 francs, while the pound cleared months-old resistance to hit $1.4448. \par \par \uc2  Much of this has been down to the non-appearance of a U.S. economic recovery and the impression this is giving that maybe the administration might not mind a softer dollar. \par \par \uc2  Both the White House and the Treasury moved to publicly deny such speculation on Thursday but a television appearance by the person who matters, Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill, backfired. \par \par \uc2  News that he was to appear on the programme bred all sorts of wild rumours in the market that maybe he was going to back away from the strong-dollar policy or even formally abandon it, causing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall sharply. \par \par \uc2  In the event he hardly mentioned the dollar, saying only that it was best not to talk about it and that the U.S. had a "continuous" dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  By this time the market was fraught enough to read his reluctance as some sort of hidden message. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar was what everyone wanted to hear about and yet he just brushed it aside, didn't even use the word 'strong'," complained a U.S. bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "It may sound like semantics, but the market's got used to the word over the last few years and when this Treasury secretary stops using it, they begin to wonder." ($1=119.89 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010816dx8g00bkt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
