\par\par \b \uc2  Ex-MOF Nakahira: Don't Think Japan-US  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Move Imminent \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2001\par 12:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. aren't likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell yen right away, but joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is always possible, a former top Japanese Finance Ministry official said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it will happen tomorrow, but depending on market conditions, its not impossible," Kosuke Nakahira, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, told Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo opposes this week's sharp yen rise, while the U.S. continues to back a strong dollar, Nakahira said in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Despite market doubts about Washington's commitment to its strong-dollar policy, he said, there's no way the U.S. would want a weaker dollar because it could undermine investors' confidence in U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "In that sense, monetary authorities of both countries are facing the same direction," Nakahira said. \par \par \uc2  He was speaking after the dollar plunged four yen in two days despite a monetary easing by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira said monetary authorities should not stand by and let current movements continue because the dollar's fall is undoubtedly speculative. \par \par \uc2  "Looking at the past two days, there has been absolutely no new incentives to either sell dollars or buy yen," Nakahira said. "Monetary authorities in Japan and the U.S. must give a signal to the market that recent moves are unjustified." \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry and the U.S. Treasury should first fire verbal warning shots, he said. If that doesn't bring the market to its senses, the authorities should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Indeed, the jawboning began Thursday, when Nakahira's current successor, Haruhiko Kuroda, warned that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Kuroda told Dow Jones Newswires the rise was inappropriate and the ministry would take action as needed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which rose as high as Y123.18 on Tuesday, lost more than four yen to hit a low Y119.08 in New York on Wednesday. As of 0835 GMT Thursday, the dollar was at Y119.60. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira said the Bank of Japan's surprise monetary easing on Tuesday was effective in showing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to providing more liquidity to the financial sector. \par \par \uc2  But he said that the action only has limited effects. Banks, under pressure to dispose of their bad loans, are unwilling to lend to firms other than the most creditworthy. \par \par \uc2  "The effect from the move is nothing other than psychological," he said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ board surprised markets by voting to raise its target for bank reserves to Y6 trillion from Y5 trillion reflecting a concern over falling exports, stubborn deflation and tanking stocks. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also agreed to increase its purchases of long-term Japanese government bonds in the market to Y600 billion a month from Y400 billion. \par \par \uc2  Nakahira added that while monetary easing would definitely not be a reason to push up the yen's value, "it won't automatically result in a weaker yen either, given current market conditions." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While flooding banks with cash would often prompt investors to sell\par yen and buy more foreign assets, that isn't happening now as the U.S.\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  looks somewhat unpalatable, he said.\par   -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010816dx8g000ka\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdr